        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0019', 1, 'Let A={1/2, 1/4, 1/6, …, 1/22} and B={1/4, 1/6, 1/8, …, 1/22}. Find A − B.', '{}', '{1/22, 1/20, 1/18, 1/16, 1/14, 1/12, 1/10, 1/8, 1/6, 1/4, 1/2}', '{1/4, 1/6, 1/8, 1/10, 1/12, 1/14, 1/16, 1/18, 1/20, 1/22}', '{1/2}', 'd', 1, 'Identify which listed reciprocal belongs only to A, not to B.', 'A contains the reciprocals of the even integers from 2 through 22; B contains those from 4 through 22. Therefore the only element in A not in B is 1/2, so A−B={1/2}. The other options respectively arise from reversing the subtraction, taking the union, or taking the intersection. Wrong-option audit: Option A: reversing A−B to B−A.  Option B: combining both sets instead of subtracting.  Option C: keeping only the common elements.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0020', 1, 'Let A={1/2, 1/4, 1/6, …, 1/24} and B={1/4, 1/6, 1/8, …, 1/24}. Find A − B.', '{1/24, 1/22, 1/20, 1/18, 1/16, 1/14, 1/12, 1/10, 1/8, 1/6, 1/4, 1/2}', '{1/4, 1/6, 1/8, 1/10, 1/12, 1/14, 1/16, 1/18, 1/20, 1/22, 1/24}', '{1/2}', '{}', 'c', 1, 'Identify which listed reciprocal belongs only to A, not to B.', 'A contains the reciprocals of the even integers from 2 through 24; B contains those from 4 through 24. Therefore the only element in A not in B is 1/2, so A−B={1/2}. The other options respectively arise from reversing the subtraction, taking the union, or taking the intersection. Wrong-option audit: Option A: reversing A−B to B−A.  Option B: combining both sets instead of subtracting.  Option D: keeping only the common elements.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0021', 1, 'In a data-sorting activity, four groups of labels are represented by A, B, C and D. For A={1, 2, 3}, B={3, 4, 5}, C={5, 6, 7}, D={7, 8, 9}, what is the required union A∪B?', '{1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9}', '{3}', '{3, 4, 5, 7, 8, 9}', '{1, 2, 3, 4, 5}', 'd', 1, 'For a union, collect every distinct element from each named set once.', 'List the elements appearing in the named sets and remove duplicates. The required union is {1, 2, 3, 4, 5}. The distractors represent combining all four sets, taking an intersection where applicable, or forming a different union. Wrong-option audit: Option A: using the wrong union scope.  Option B: taking an intersection.  Option C: using a different set combination.'),
