        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0110', 1, 'In a rational-number classification exercise, a relation is defined by an integer difference. On Q, define R by aRb iff a−b∈Z. For a=1/2 and b=5/2, which statement about the relation''s classification is consistent with the chapter?', 'Symmetric only', 'Transitive only', 'Equivalence relation', 'Reflexive only', 'd', 3, 'Check the three properties separately: reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.', 'Here a−b=2, an integer, so the pair is related. The chapter proves this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive; therefore it is an equivalence relation. The other options identify only one property and omit the combined classification. Wrong-option audit: Option A: checking only reflexivity.  Option B: checking only symmetry.  Option C: checking only transitivity.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0111', 1, 'A teacher uses rational numbers to illustrate an equivalence relation. On Q, define R by aRb iff a−b∈Z. For a=2/3 and b=8/3, which statement about the relation''s classification is consistent with the chapter?', 'Transitive only', 'Equivalence relation', 'Reflexive only', 'Symmetric only', 'c', 3, 'Check the three properties separately: reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.', 'Here a−b=2, an integer, so the pair is related. The chapter proves this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive; therefore it is an equivalence relation. The other options identify only one property and omit the combined classification. Wrong-option audit: Option A: checking only reflexivity.  Option B: checking only symmetry.  Option D: checking only transitivity.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0112', 1, 'In a number-system worksheet, students test a relation on Q using differences. On Q, define R by aRb iff a−b∈Z. For a=3/4 and b=11/4, which statement about the relation''s classification is consistent with the chapter?', 'Equivalence relation', 'Reflexive only', 'Symmetric only', 'Transitive only', 'b', 3, 'Check the three properties separately: reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.', 'Here a−b=2, an integer, so the pair is related. The chapter proves this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive; therefore it is an equivalence relation. The other options identify only one property and omit the combined classification. Wrong-option audit: Option A: checking only reflexivity.  Option C: checking only symmetry.  Option D: checking only transitivity.'),
