        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0119', 1, 'On Q, define R by aRb iff a−b∈Z. For a=11/6 and b=29/6, which statement about the relation''s classification is consistent with the chapter?', 'Transitive only', 'Equivalence relation', 'Reflexive only', 'Symmetric only', 'c', 3, 'Check the three properties separately: reflexive, symmetric, and transitive.', 'Here a−b=3, an integer, so the pair is related. The chapter proves this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive; therefore it is an equivalence relation. The other options identify only one property and omit the combined classification. Wrong-option audit: Option A: checking only reflexivity.  Option B: checking only symmetry.  Option D: checking only transitivity.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0120', 1, 'On Q, define R by aRb iff a−b∈Z. For a=1/2 and b=5/2, which classification is established for this relation in the chapter?', 'Symmetric only', 'Transitive only', 'Equivalence relation', 'Reflexive only', 'd', 3, 'Check the three required properties: reflexive, symmetric and transitive.', 'For these values, a−b=2, an integer, so the pair satisfies the relation. More generally, the chapter verifies that this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive; therefore it is an equivalence relation. The alternatives name only one property. Wrong-option audit: Option A: checking only reflexivity.  Option B: checking only symmetry.  Option C: checking only transitivity.'),
