        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0129', 1, 'On Q, define R by aRb iff a−b∈Z. For a=11/6 and b=29/6, which classification is established for this relation in the chapter?', 'Transitive only', 'Equivalence relation', 'Reflexive only', 'Symmetric only', 'c', 3, 'Check the three required properties: reflexive, symmetric and transitive.', 'For these values, a−b=3, an integer, so the pair satisfies the relation. More generally, the chapter verifies that this relation is reflexive, symmetric and transitive; therefore it is an equivalence relation. The alternatives name only one property. Wrong-option audit: Option A: checking only reflexivity.  Option B: checking only symmetry.  Option D: checking only transitivity.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0130', 1, 'In an algebra worksheet, two ordered pairs are declared equal. If (x+2, 4) = (6, y+1), determine x and y.', '(4,3)', '(4,5)', '(8,3)', '(-4,3)', 'a', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+2=6, giving x=4; and 4=y+1, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option B: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option C: adding the constant to the second component.  Option D: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0131', 1, 'A student solves for two unknown components from an equality of ordered pairs. If (x+3, 5) = (7, y+2), determine x and y.', '(4,7)', '(10,3)', '(-4,3)', '(4,3)', 'd', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+3=7, giving x=4; and 5=y+2, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option A: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option B: adding the constant to the second component.  Option C: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0132', 1, 'In a coordinate-pair exercise, corresponding components must be matched. If (x+4, 6) = (8, y+3), determine x and y.', '(12,3)', '(-4,3)', '(4,3)', '(4,9)', 'c', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+4=8, giving x=4; and 6=y+3, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option A: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option B: adding the constant to the second component.  Option D: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0133', 1, 'If (x+5, 7) = (9, y+4), determine x and y.', '(-4,3)', '(4,3)', '(4,11)', '(14,3)', 'b', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+5=9, giving x=4; and 7=y+4, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option A: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option C: adding the constant to the second component.  Option D: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0134', 1, 'If (x+6, 8) = (10, y+5), determine x and y.', '(4,3)', '(4,13)', '(16,3)', '(-4,3)', 'a', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+6=10, giving x=4; and 8=y+5, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option B: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option C: adding the constant to the second component.  Option D: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0135', 1, 'If (x+7, 9) = (11, y+6), determine x and y.', '(4,15)', '(18,3)', '(-4,3)', '(4,3)', 'd', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+7=11, giving x=4; and 9=y+6, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option A: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option B: adding the constant to the second component.  Option C: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0136', 1, 'If (x+8, 10) = (12, y+7), determine x and y.', '(20,3)', '(-4,3)', '(4,3)', '(4,17)', 'c', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+8=12, giving x=4; and 10=y+7, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option A: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option B: adding the constant to the second component.  Option D: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0137', 1, 'If (x+9, 11) = (13, y+8), determine x and y.', '(-4,3)', '(4,3)', '(4,19)', '(22,3)', 'b', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+9=13, giving x=4; and 11=y+8, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option A: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option C: adding the constant to the second component.  Option D: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0138', 1, 'If (x+10, 12) = (14, y+9), determine x and y.', '(4,3)', '(4,21)', '(24,3)', '(-4,3)', 'a', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+10=14, giving x=4; and 12=y+9, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option B: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option C: adding the constant to the second component.  Option D: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0139', 1, 'If (x+11, 13) = (15, y+10), determine x and y.', '(4,23)', '(26,3)', '(-4,3)', '(4,3)', 'd', 1, 'Equate the first components and second components separately.', 'Equate corresponding components: x+11=15, giving x=4; and 13=y+10, giving y=3. Thus (x,y)=(4,3). The distractors result from adding the constant to y, adding instead of subtracting in x, or reversing the subtraction. Wrong-option audit: Option A: adding instead of subtracting a constant.  Option B: adding the constant to the second component.  Option C: reversing a subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0140', 1, 'In a pairing-table activity, every element of A is paired with every element of A. For A={1, 2}, what is n(A×A)?', '2', '5', '3', '4', 'd', 1, 'Multiply the number of elements in A by itself.', 'n(A)=2, so n(A×A)=n(A)×n(A)=2×2=4. The other values arise from using n(A), doubling it, or an incomplete count. The replacement is an off-by-one/incomplete count. Wrong-option audit: Option A: using n(A) instead of n(A)².  Option B: counting only one ordering.  Option C: stopping before all pairings are included.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0141', 1, 'A teacher asks students to construct a complete self-Cartesian product. Write A×A when A={2, 3, 4}.', '{(2,2), (3,3), (4,4)}', '{(2,2), (2,3), (2,4), (3,3), (3,4), (4,4)}', '{(2,2), (2,3), (2,4), (3,2), (3,3), (3,4), (4,2), (4,3), (4,4)}', '{(2,3), (2,4), (3,2), (3,4), (4,2), (4,3)}', 'c', 2, 'Pair every element of A with every element of A, preserving order.', 'Cartesian product A×A contains every ordered pair whose two components are chosen from A. Therefore the full set is {(2,2), (2,3), (2,4), (3,2), (3,3), (3,4), (4,2), (4,3), (4,4)}. The distractors omit equal-component pairs, retain only the diagonal, or keep only one ordering. Wrong-option audit: Option A: using n(A) instead of n(A)².  Option B: counting only one ordering.  Option D: stopping before all pairings are included.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0142', 1, 'In a classroom table exercise, A×A is being listed. For A={3, 4, 5, 6}, what is n(A×A)?', '7', '16', '4', '8', 'b', 1, 'Multiply the number of elements in A by itself.', 'n(A)=4, so n(A×A)=n(A)×n(A)=4×4=16. The other values arise from using n(A), doubling it, or an incomplete count. Wrong-option audit: Option A: using n(A) instead of n(A)².  Option C: counting only one ordering.  Option D: stopping before all pairings are included.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0143', 1, 'Write A×A when A={4, 5, 6, 7, 8}.', '{(4,4), (4,5), (4,6), (4,7), (4,8), (5,4), (5,5), (5,6), (5,7), (5,8), (6,4), (6,5), (6,6), (6,7), (6,8), (7,4), (7,5), (7,6), (7,7), (7,8), (8,4), (8,5), (8,6), (8,7), (8,8)}', '{(4,5), (4,6), (4,7), (4,8), (5,4), (5,6), (5,7), (5,8), (6,4), (6,5), (6,7), (6,8), (7,4), (7,5), (7,6), (7,8), (8,4), (8,5), (8,6), (8,7)}', '{(4,4), (5,5), (6,6), (7,7), (8,8)}', '{(4,4), (4,5), (4,6), (4,7), (4,8), (5,5), (5,6), (5,7), (5,8), (6,6), (6,7), (6,8), (7,7), (7,8), (8,8)}', 'a', 2, 'Pair every element of A with every element of A, preserving order.', 'Cartesian product A×A contains every ordered pair whose two components are chosen from A. Therefore the full set is {(4,4), (4,5), (4,6), (4,7), (4,8), (5,4), (5,5), (5,6), (5,7), (5,8), (6,4), (6,5), (6,6), (6,7), (6,8), (7,4), (7,5), (7,6), (7,7), (7,8), (8,4), (8,5), (8,6), (8,7), (8,8)}. The distractors omit equal-component pairs, retain only the diagonal, or keep only one ordering. Wrong-option audit: Option B: using n(A) instead of n(A)².  Option C: counting only one ordering.  Option D: stopping before all pairings are included.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0144', 1, 'For A={5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10}, what is n(A×A)?', '6', '12', '11', '36', 'd', 1, 'Multiply the number of elements in A by itself.', 'n(A)=6, so n(A×A)=n(A)×n(A)=6×6=36. The other values arise from using n(A), doubling it, or an incomplete count. Wrong-option audit: Option A: using n(A) instead of n(A)².  Option B: counting only one ordering.  Option C: stopping before all pairings are included.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0145', 1, 'Write A×A when A={6, 7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12}.', '{(6,6), (7,7), (8,8), (9,9), (10,10), (11,11), (12,12)}', '{(6,6), (6,7), (6,8), (6,9), (6,10), (6,11), (6,12), (7,7), (7,8), (7,9), (7,10), (7,11), (7,12), (8,8), (8,9), (8,10), (8,11), (8,12), (9,9), (9,10), (9,11), (9,12), (10,10), (10,11), (10,12), (11,11), (11,12), (12,12)}', '{(6,6), (6,7), (6,8), (6,9), (6,10), (6,11), (6,12), (7,6), (7,7), (7,8), (7,9), (7,10), (7,11), (7,12), (8,6), (8,7), (8,8), (8,9), (8,10), (8,11), (8,12), (9,6), (9,7), (9,8), (9,9), (9,10), (9,11), (9,12), (10,6), (10,7), (10,8), (10,9), (10,10), (10,11), (10,12), (11,6), (11,7), (11,8), (11,9), (11,10), (11,11), (11,12), (12,6), (12,7), (12,8), (12,9), (12,10), (12,11), (12,12)}', '{(6,7), (6,8), (6,9), (6,10), (6,11), (6,12), (7,6), (7,8), (7,9), (7,10), (7,11), (7,12), (8,6), (8,7), (8,9), (8,10), (8,11), (8,12), (9,6), (9,7), (9,8), (9,10), (9,11), (9,12), (10,6), (10,7), (10,8), (10,9), (10,11), (10,12), (11,6), (11,7), (11,8), (11,9), (11,10), (11,12), (12,6), (12,7), (12,8), (12,9), (12,10), (12,11)}', 'c', 2, 'Pair every element of A with every element of A, preserving order.', 'Cartesian product A×A contains every ordered pair whose two components are chosen from A. Therefore the full set is {(6,6), (6,7), (6,8), (6,9), (6,10), (6,11), (6,12), (7,6), (7,7), (7,8), (7,9), (7,10), (7,11), (7,12), (8,6), (8,7), (8,8), (8,9), (8,10), (8,11), (8,12), (9,6), (9,7), (9,8), (9,9), (9,10), (9,11), (9,12), (10,6), (10,7), (10,8), (10,9), (10,10), (10,11), (10,12), (11,6), (11,7), (11,8), (11,9), (11,10), (11,11), (11,12), (12,6), (12,7), (12,8), (12,9), (12,10), (12,11), (12,12)}. The distractors omit equal-component pairs, retain only the diagonal, or keep only one ordering. Wrong-option audit: Option A: using n(A) instead of n(A)².  Option B: counting only one ordering.  Option D: stopping before all pairings are included.'),
        ('mth11a-01-mcq-0146', 1, 'For A={7, 8, 9, 10, 11, 12, 13, 14}, what is n(A×A)?', '15', '64', '8', '16', 'b', 1, 'Multiply the number of elements in A by itself.', 'n(A)=8, so n(A×A)=n(A)×n(A)=8×8=64. The other values arise from using n(A), doubling it, or an incomplete count. Wrong-option audit: Option A: using n(A) instead of n(A)².  Option C: counting only one ordering.  Option D: stopping before all pairings are included.'),
