        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0006', 6, 'Three points are collinear when their determinant is:', '0', '1', 'positive', '−1', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'Zero determinant means zero area and collinearity. Option B: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option C: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option D: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0007', 6, 'If the determinant for three points is zero, their triangle area is:', 'positive', '0', '−1', '1', 'b', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'Zero determinant gives zero triangle area. Option A: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option C: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option D: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0008', 6, 'Find |-2 2 3; -2 2 3; -9 -6 9|.', '-3', '0', '3', '1', 'b', 1, 'Check whether two rows or columns are identical.', 'The first two rows are identical, so the determinant is 0. Option A: adding a row and reversing its sign. Option C: adding a row instead of using the property. Option D: treating identical rows as independent.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0009', 6, 'Using a determinant, determine whether A(2, -3), B(0, -1), and C(-2, 1) are collinear.', 'Not collinear', 'Nonzero area', 'Collinear', 'Only two points are collinear', 'c', 2, 'Set the 3×3 collinearity determinant equal to zero.', 'The determinant is 0, so the triangle area is zero and the three points are collinear. Option A: obtaining a nonzero determinant through an arithmetic error. Option B: forgetting that a zero determinant gives zero area. Option D: misinterpreting the determinant criterion.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0010', 6, 'Find the area of the triangle with vertices A(-6, -6), B(6, -6), and C(3, 4).', '120', '6', '-60', '60', 'd', 2, 'Use the determinant area formula and take the absolute value.', 'Twice the area is 120; therefore area = ½(120) = 60 square units. Option A: forgetting the factor 1/2. Option B: using the wrong sign for one y-coordinate. Option C: using a signed determinant directly as area.'),
