        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0108', 6, 'Using a determinant, determine whether A(-3, 0), B(-4, -4), and C(-6, -12) are collinear.', 'Not collinear', 'Only two points are collinear', 'Nonzero area', 'Collinear', 'd', 2, 'Set the 3×3 collinearity determinant equal to zero.', 'The determinant is 0, so the triangle area is zero and the three points are collinear. Option A: obtaining a nonzero determinant through an arithmetic error. Option B: misinterpreting the determinant criterion. Option C: forgetting that a zero determinant gives zero area.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0109', 6, 'Evaluate |-3 6 0; -2 2 7; -5 3 -1|.', '-153', '267', '291', '141', 'a', 2, 'Expand along the first row using +, −, + cofactors.', 'D = -3(2·-1−7·3) − 6(-2·-1−7·-5) + 0(-2·3−2·-5) = -153. Option B: using the wrong sign for the third cofactor. Option C: using + for the middle cofactor. Option D: adding terms inside the 2×2 minors.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0110', 6, 'Determine whether 1x + 1y + (-2) = 0; 2x + -1y + (-10) = 0; 3x + 2y + (-8) = 0 are consistent.', 'Not consistent', 'Consistent', 'Parallel only', 'No common solution', 'b', 2, 'Form the 3×3 determinant from a, b and c.', 'The determinant of the coefficient-constant rows is 0, so the necessary consistency condition is satisfied. Option A: assuming a nonzero determinant. Option C: using an unrelated slope conclusion. Option D: confusing the determinant test.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0111', 6, 'If k times one row is added to another, the determinant:', 'remains unchanged', 'becomes zero', 'changes sign', 'is multiplied by k', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'Adding a multiple of another row preserves the determinant. Option B: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option C: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option D: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0112', 6, 'What does a₃₁ denote?', '3rd row, 1st column', '2nd row, 1st column', '1st row, 3rd column', '3rd column, 1st row', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'The first subscript is the row and the second is the column. Option B: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option C: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option D: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0113', 6, 'Using a determinant, determine whether A(0, 1), B(-4, 0), and C(4, 2) are collinear.', 'Only two points are collinear', 'Nonzero area', 'Collinear', 'Not collinear', 'c', 2, 'Set the 3×3 collinearity determinant equal to zero.', 'The determinant is 0, so the triangle area is zero and the three points are collinear. Option A: misinterpreting the determinant criterion. Option B: forgetting that a zero determinant gives zero area. Option D: obtaining a nonzero determinant through an arithmetic error.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0114', 6, 'What is the necessary determinant condition for consistency?', 'D ≠ 0', 'D = 0', 'D < 0', 'D = 1', 'b', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'The necessary condition stated is D = 0. Option A: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option C: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option D: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0115', 6, 'What does R₁ → R₁ + kR₂ mean?', 'Multiply R₁ by kR₂', 'Replace R₂ by R₁ + kR₂', 'Add kR₂ to R₁', 'Subtract kR₁ from R₂', 'c', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'The operation adds k times R₂ to R₁. Option A: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option B: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option D: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0116', 6, 'How many elements are in a determinant of order 3?', '12', '6', '9', '8', 'c', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'A 3×3 determinant contains 9 elements. Option A: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option B: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option D: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0117', 6, 'Solve using Cramer’s Rule: 3x + 1y + -2z = 11; 1x + 2y + 1z = 2; 2x + -1y + 3z = -3.', '(x, y, z) = (-2, 1, -2)', '(x, y, z) = (1, 2, -2)', '(x, y, z) = (2, 1, -2)', '(x, y, z) = (2, -2, 1)', 'c', 3, 'Form D, Dx, Dy and Dz, then use x = Dx/D, y = Dy/D and z = Dz/D.', 'D = 30 ≠ 0. The Cramer ratios give x = 2, y = 1, z = -2. Option A: reversing the sign of x. Option B: interchanging the x and y determinant ratios. Option D: interchanging the y and z ratios.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0118', 6, 'Find |9 7 -4; 9 7 -4; 7 -3 -4|.', '12', '0', '-12', '1', 'b', 1, 'Check whether two rows or columns are identical.', 'The first two rows are identical, so the determinant is 0. Option A: adding a row instead of using the property. Option C: adding a row and reversing its sign. Option D: treating identical rows as independent.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0119', 6, 'Determine whether 1x + 1y + (-4) = 0; 2x + -1y + (1) = 0; 3x + 2y + (-9) = 0 are consistent.', 'Consistent', 'Not consistent', 'Parallel only', 'No common solution', 'a', 2, 'Form the 3×3 determinant from a, b and c.', 'The determinant of the coefficient-constant rows is 0, so the necessary consistency condition is satisfied. Option B: assuming a nonzero determinant. Option C: using an unrelated slope conclusion. Option D: confusing the determinant test.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0120', 6, 'Using a determinant, determine whether A(-2, 4), B(5, 5), and C(-9, 3) are collinear.', 'Collinear', 'Nonzero area', 'Only two points are collinear', 'Not collinear', 'a', 2, 'Set the 3×3 collinearity determinant equal to zero.', 'The determinant is 0, so the triangle area is zero and the three points are collinear. Option B: forgetting that a zero determinant gives zero area. Option C: misinterpreting the determinant criterion. Option D: obtaining a nonzero determinant through an arithmetic error.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0121', 6, 'Evaluate |-3 6 -5; 4 5 -1; 7 3 0|.', '148', '64', '-184', '-82', 'b', 2, 'Expand along the first row using +, −, + cofactors.', 'D = -3(5·0−-1·3) − 6(4·0−-1·7) + -5(4·3−5·7) = 64. Option A: using + for the middle cofactor. Option C: adding terms inside the 2×2 minors. Option D: using the wrong sign for the third cofactor.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0122', 6, 'A determinant has value 92. If every element of one row is multiplied by 2, what is the new value?', '92', '186', '-184', '184', 'd', 1, 'A factor taken from one row multiplies the determinant by the same factor.', 'New determinant = 2(92) = 184. Option A: forgetting the row factor. Option B: adding the factor instead of multiplying. Option C: using the wrong sign for the factor.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0123', 6, 'Using a determinant, determine whether A(-2, -4), B(-4, 0), and C(0, -8) are collinear.', 'Collinear', 'Not collinear', 'Nonzero area', 'Only two points are collinear', 'a', 2, 'Set the 3×3 collinearity determinant equal to zero.', 'The determinant is 0, so the triangle area is zero and the three points are collinear. Option B: obtaining a nonzero determinant through an arithmetic error. Option C: forgetting that a zero determinant gives zero area. Option D: misinterpreting the determinant criterion.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0124', 6, 'Solve using Cramer’s Rule: 2x + -1y + 1z = 1; 1x + 2y + 3z = 8; 3x + 1y + -4z = 1.', '(x, y, z) = (2, 1, 1)', '(x, y, z) = (1, 2, 1)', '(x, y, z) = (-1, 2, 1)', '(x, y, z) = (1, 1, 2)', 'b', 3, 'Form D, Dx, Dy and Dz, then use x = Dx/D, y = Dy/D and z = Dz/D.', 'D = -40 ≠ 0. The Cramer ratios give x = 1, y = 2, z = 1. Option A: interchanging the x and y determinant ratios. Option C: reversing the sign of x. Option D: interchanging the y and z ratios.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0125', 6, 'A determinant has value -178. If every element of one row is multiplied by 2, what is the new value?', '356', '-354', '-356', '-178', 'c', 1, 'A factor taken from one row multiplies the determinant by the same factor.', 'New determinant = 2(-178) = -356. Option A: using the wrong sign for the factor. Option B: adding the factor instead of multiplying. Option D: forgetting the row factor.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0126', 6, 'A determinant has value -34. If every element of one row is multiplied by 2, what is the new value?', '-68', '68', '-66', '-34', 'a', 1, 'A factor taken from one row multiplies the determinant by the same factor.', 'New determinant = 2(-34) = -68. Option B: using the wrong sign for the factor. Option C: adding the factor instead of multiplying. Option D: forgetting the row factor.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0127', 6, 'Find the area of the triangle with vertices A(0, 3), B(-2, 4), and C(-3, 6).', '3', '-3/2', '3/2', '27/2', 'c', 2, 'Use the determinant area formula and take the absolute value.', 'Twice the area is 3; therefore area = ½(3) = 3/2 square units. Option A: forgetting the factor 1/2. Option B: using a signed determinant directly as area. Option D: using the wrong sign for one y-coordinate.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0128', 6, 'Determine whether 1x + 1y + (-3) = 0; 2x + -1y + (3) = 0; 3x + 2y + (-6) = 0 are consistent.', 'Not consistent', 'Parallel only', 'Consistent', 'No common solution', 'c', 2, 'Form the 3×3 determinant from a, b and c.', 'The determinant of the coefficient-constant rows is 0, so the necessary consistency condition is satisfied. Option A: assuming a nonzero determinant. Option B: using an unrelated slope conclusion. Option D: confusing the determinant test.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0129', 6, 'Which ratio gives y?', 'Dy/D', 'Dx/D', 'Dz/D', 'D/Dy', 'a', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'Cramer’s Rule gives y = Dy/D. Option B: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option C: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option D: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0130', 6, 'Find |5 2 −1; 0 8 3; 0 0 -3|.', '10', '-120', '120', '37', 'b', 1, 'All entries below the main diagonal are zero.', 'D = (5)(8)(-3) = -120. Option A: adding diagonal entries. Option C: introducing an unnecessary sign change. Option D: stopping after a partial product.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0131', 6, 'A determinant has value -59. If R₁ is replaced by R₁ + (2)R₂, what is the new value?', '-59', '-57', '-118', '59', 'a', 1, 'Adding a multiple of one row to another preserves the determinant.', 'The operation preserves the determinant, so the new value remains -59. Option B: adding the multiplier directly to the determinant. Option C: multiplying instead of adding a row multiple. Option D: changing sign without a row interchange.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0132', 6, 'Determine whether 1x + 1y + (4) = 0; 2x + -1y + (5) = 0; 3x + 2y + (11) = 0 are consistent.', 'Consistent', 'Parallel only', 'No common solution', 'Not consistent', 'a', 2, 'Form the 3×3 determinant from a, b and c.', 'The determinant of the coefficient-constant rows is 0, so the necessary consistency condition is satisfied. Option B: using an unrelated slope conclusion. Option C: confusing the determinant test. Option D: assuming a nonzero determinant.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0133', 6, 'Using the row-splitting property, evaluate |4 10 12; 2 3 4; 1 0 2|.', '-11', '-45', '-18', '-12', 'd', 1, 'Write the first row as the sum of two rows and split the determinant.', 'D = -15 + (3) = -12. Option A: adding an extra constant. Option B: multiplying the two component determinants. Option C: subtracting the component determinants.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0134', 6, 'Using the row-splitting property, evaluate |12 8 8; 2 3 4; 1 0 2|.', '48', '-24', '432', '49', 'a', 1, 'Write the first row as the sum of two rows and split the determinant.', 'D = 12 + (36) = 48. Option B: subtracting the component determinants. Option C: multiplying the two component determinants. Option D: adding an extra constant.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0135', 6, 'Determine whether 1x + 1y + (-1) = 0; 2x + -1y + (-2) = 0; 3x + 2y + (-3) = 0 are consistent.', 'No common solution', 'Not consistent', 'Parallel only', 'Consistent', 'd', 2, 'Form the 3×3 determinant from a, b and c.', 'The determinant of the coefficient-constant rows is 0, so the necessary consistency condition is satisfied. Option A: confusing the determinant test. Option B: assuming a nonzero determinant. Option C: using an unrelated slope conclusion.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0136', 6, 'Determine whether 1x + 1y + (5) = 0; 2x + -1y + (4) = 0; 3x + 2y + (13) = 0 are consistent.', 'No common solution', 'Consistent', 'Parallel only', 'Not consistent', 'b', 2, 'Form the 3×3 determinant from a, b and c.', 'The determinant of the coefficient-constant rows is 0, so the necessary consistency condition is satisfied. Option A: confusing the determinant test. Option C: using an unrelated slope conclusion. Option D: assuming a nonzero determinant.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0137', 6, 'What factor multiplies the coordinate determinant for triangle area?', '1/3', '1', '2', '1/2', 'd', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'The triangle area formula contains the factor 1/2. Option A: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option B: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option C: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0138', 6, 'A determinant has value -11. If R₁ is replaced by R₁ + (2)R₂, what is the new value?', '-9', '-22', '11', '-11', 'd', 1, 'Adding a multiple of one row to another preserves the determinant.', 'The operation preserves the determinant, so the new value remains -11. Option A: adding the multiplier directly to the determinant. Option B: multiplying instead of adding a row multiple. Option C: changing sign without a row interchange.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0139', 6, 'Without expanding, find |4 7 2; 4 7 2; 4 1 5|.', '-10', '10', '1', '0', 'd', 1, 'Look for a determinant property that makes expansion unnecessary.', 'Two rows are identical, so the determinant is 0. Option A: adding the third row and reversing its sign. Option B: adding the third row. Option C: treating the repeated rows as nonzero independent rows.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0140', 6, 'Determine whether 1x + 1y + (6) = 0; 2x + -1y + (3) = 0; 3x + 2y + (15) = 0 are consistent.', 'Parallel only', 'Not consistent', 'Consistent', 'No common solution', 'c', 2, 'Form the 3×3 determinant from a, b and c.', 'The determinant of the coefficient-constant rows is 0, so the necessary consistency condition is satisfied. Option A: using an unrelated slope conclusion. Option B: assuming a nonzero determinant. Option D: confusing the determinant test.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0141', 6, 'Determine whether 1x + 1y + (-1) = 0; 2x + -1y + (1) = 0; 3x + 2y + (-2) = 0 are consistent.', 'Parallel only', 'Not consistent', 'No common solution', 'Consistent', 'd', 2, 'Form the 3×3 determinant from a, b and c.', 'The determinant of the coefficient-constant rows is 0, so the necessary consistency condition is satisfied. Option A: using an unrelated slope conclusion. Option B: assuming a nonzero determinant. Option C: confusing the determinant test.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0142', 6, 'A determinant has value -95. If R₁ is replaced by R₁ + (3)R₂, what is the new value?', '-95', '-92', '95', '-285', 'a', 1, 'Adding a multiple of one row to another preserves the determinant.', 'The operation preserves the determinant, so the new value remains -95. Option B: adding the multiplier directly to the determinant. Option C: changing sign without a row interchange. Option D: multiplying instead of adding a row multiple.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0143', 6, 'Find the area of the triangle with vertices A(-5, -6), B(-3, 4), and C(-2, -1).', '2', '20', '10', '-10', 'c', 2, 'Use the determinant area formula and take the absolute value.', 'Twice the area is 20; therefore area = ½(20) = 10 square units. Option A: using the wrong sign for one y-coordinate. Option B: forgetting the factor 1/2. Option D: using a signed determinant directly as area.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0144', 6, 'Why is absolute value used in triangle area?', 'Coordinates are positive', 'Determinants are always positive', 'The factor 1/2 is negative', 'Area is positive', 'd', 1, 'Recall the stated determinant property or formula.', 'Area is positive, so the absolute value is taken. Option A: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option B: using the wrong determinant rule or definition. Option C: using the wrong determinant rule or definition.'),
        ('mth11a-06-mcq-0145', 6, 'Determine whether 3x + 1y + (2) = 0; 4x + 1y + (1) = 0; 7x + 2y + (4) = 0 are consistent.', 'Consistent', 'Not consistent', 'Infinitely many solutions', 'Exactly one common solution', 'b', 2, 'Evaluate the determinant of the coefficient-constant rows.', 'D = -1 ≠ 0, so the necessary condition D = 0 is not satisfied. Option A: ignoring that the determinant is nonzero. Option C: assuming dependent equations without checking D. Option D: using a nonzero determinant as a consistency criterion.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0001', 7, 'Evaluate lim(r→2) [(4/3)πr²].', '4π', '8/3π', '12π', '16/3π', 'd', 1, 'Use direct substitution because the expression is defined at the approach value.', 'Substitute r=2: (4/3)π(2)² = 16/3π. Option A: inverted the coefficient 4/3 and shifted the result by one. Option B: used r instead of r². Option C: substituted 3 instead of 2.'),
