        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0169', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [log(1+9x)/x]', '9', '1/9', '81', '10', 'a', 2, 'Rewrite the denominator as (9x) and use lim(t→0) log(1+t)/t=1.', 'log(1+9x)/x = 9[log(1+9x)/(9x)], so the limit is 9. Option B: inverted the multiplier c. Option C: used c² instead of c. Option D: added 1 to the multiplier.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0170', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [log(1+10x)/x]', '10', '1/10', '11', '100', 'a', 2, 'Rewrite the denominator as (10x) and use lim(t→0) log(1+t)/t=1.', 'log(1+10x)/x = 10[log(1+10x)/(10x)], so the limit is 10. Option B: inverted the multiplier c. Option C: added 1 to the multiplier. Option D: used c² instead of c.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0171', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((8)^x − 2^x − 4^x + 1)/x²]', '(log 2)/(log 4)', '(log 2)(log 4)', 'log(6)', 'log(8)', 'b', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (2^x−1)(4^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 2)(log 4). Option A: divided the two logarithmic limits. Option C: added the logarithm arguments. Option D: combined the two logarithms into one.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0172', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((15)^x − 3^x − 5^x + 1)/x²]', '(log 3)(log 5)', 'log(8)', '(log 3)/(log 5)', 'log(15)', 'a', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (3^x−1)(5^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 3)(log 5). Option B: added the logarithm arguments. Option C: divided the two logarithmic limits. Option D: combined the two logarithms into one.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0173', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((24)^x − 4^x − 6^x + 1)/x²]', '(log 4)(log 6)', 'log(10)', 'log(24)', '(log 4)/(log 6)', 'a', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (4^x−1)(6^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 4)(log 6). Option B: added the logarithm arguments. Option C: combined the two logarithms into one. Option D: divided the two logarithmic limits.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0174', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((35)^x − 5^x − 7^x + 1)/x²]', '(log 5)/(log 7)', 'log(12)', 'log(35)', '(log 5)(log 7)', 'd', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (5^x−1)(7^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 5)(log 7). Option A: divided the two logarithmic limits. Option B: added the logarithm arguments. Option C: combined the two logarithms into one.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0175', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((48)^x − 6^x − 8^x + 1)/x²]', '(log 6)/(log 8)', 'log(14)', '(log 6)(log 8)', 'log(48)', 'c', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (6^x−1)(8^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 6)(log 8). Option A: divided the two logarithmic limits. Option B: added the logarithm arguments. Option D: combined the two logarithms into one.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0176', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((10)^x − 2^x − 5^x + 1)/x²]', '(log 2)/(log 5)', '(log 2)(log 5)', 'log(10)', 'log(7)', 'b', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (2^x−1)(5^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 2)(log 5). Option A: divided the two logarithmic limits. Option C: combined the two logarithms into one. Option D: added the logarithm arguments.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0177', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((21)^x − 3^x − 7^x + 1)/x²]', 'log(10)', '(log 3)/(log 7)', '(log 3)(log 7)', 'log(21)', 'c', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (3^x−1)(7^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 3)(log 7). Option A: added the logarithm arguments. Option B: divided the two logarithmic limits. Option D: combined the two logarithms into one.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0178', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((36)^x − 4^x − 9^x + 1)/x²]', 'log(36)', 'log(13)', '(log 4)(log 9)', '(log 4)/(log 9)', 'c', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (4^x−1)(9^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 4)(log 9). Option A: combined the two logarithms into one. Option B: added the logarithm arguments. Option D: divided the two logarithmic limits.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0179', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((40)^x − 5^x − 8^x + 1)/x²]', 'log(40)', 'log(13)', '(log 5)(log 8)', '(log 5)/(log 8)', 'c', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (5^x−1)(8^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 5)(log 8). Option A: combined the two logarithms into one. Option B: added the logarithm arguments. Option D: divided the two logarithmic limits.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0180', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0) [((54)^x − 6^x − 9^x + 1)/x²]', '(log 6)(log 9)', 'log(54)', 'log(15)', '(log 6)/(log 9)', 'a', 3, 'Rewrite ({a*b})^x as {a}^x{b}^x and factor the numerator as ({a}^x−1)({b}^x−1).', 'The numerator factors as (6^x−1)(9^x−1). Dividing by x² gives the product of the two standard limits: (log 6)(log 9). Option B: combined the two logarithms into one. Option C: added the logarithm arguments. Option D: divided the two logarithmic limits.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0181', 7, 'If x approaches 5 with δ=0.2, which condition describes the approach while excluding x=5?', '|x−5|>0.2', '0<|x−5|<0.2', '|x−5|<0.2', '|x−5|≤0.2', 'b', 1, 'Use the definition with both exclusion of x=a and a distance bound.', 'The condition is 0<|x−5|<0.2. Option A: reversed the distance condition. Option C: forgot to exclude x=5. Option D: included equality at the boundary.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0182', 7, 'For a rational function P(x)/Q(x), what condition allows direct substitution at x=a?', 'P(a)=0', 'Q(a)=0', 'P(a)≠0', 'Q(a)≠0', 'd', 1, 'Check the denominator at the approach value.', 'Direct substitution applies in the stated rational-function case when Q(a) is nonzero. Option A: checked the numerator rather than the denominator. Option B: used the indeterminate case instead. Option C: checked the wrong quantity.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0183', 7, 'If P(a)=0 and Q(a)=0 for P(x)/Q(x), which algebraic factor is cancelled when available?', 'x+a', 'a−x', 'x²−a²', 'x−a', 'd', 1, 'Use the factor theorem form shown for the zero-over-zero case.', 'When both P(a) and Q(a) are zero, x−a is a factor of both and can be cancelled for x≠a. Option A: used the wrong linear factor. Option B: changed the sign without accounting for it. Option C: used a larger factor than required.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0184', 7, 'For a rational expression, P(3)=5 and Q(3)=0. Which ordered pair correctly records these values?', '(5,1)', '(0,5)', '(3,5)', '(5,0)', 'd', 1, 'Record P(a) and Q(a) in the stated order.', 'At a=3, P(3)=5 and Q(3)=0, so the ordered pair is (5,0). Option A: replaced the zero denominator value by 1. Option B: interchanged numerator and denominator values. Option C: used the approach value instead of Q(3).'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0185', 7, 'If the left-hand and right-hand limits at x=4 are both 7, what is the two-sided limit?', '7', '0', '14', '49', 'a', 1, 'A two-sided limit exists when the two one-sided limits are equal.', 'Both one-sided limits equal 7, so the two-sided limit is 7. Option B: ignored the common one-sided value. Option C: added the two one-sided limits. Option D: multiplied the two one-sided limits.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0186', 7, 'For f(x)={2x+1 for x<2; 3x−1 for x>2}, find the left-hand limit as x approaches 2.', '5', '6', '3', '4', 'a', 1, 'Use the branch valid for x<2 and substitute x=2.', 'The left-hand branch is 2x+1, so at x=2 the left-hand limit is 2(2)+1=5. Option B: used the right-hand branch. Option C: substituted only the constant term. Option D: used 2x−1 instead of 2x+1.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0187', 7, 'If lim f(x)=3 and lim g(x)=−2 as x approaches a, what is lim[f(x)+g(x)]?', '5', '−6', '−1', '1', 'd', 1, 'Use the sum/difference algebra of limits.', 'lim[f+g]=3+(−2)=1. Option A: added absolute values. Option B: used multiplication instead of addition. Option C: reversed the order as 2−3.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0188', 7, 'If lim f(x)=4 and lim g(x)=−3 as x approaches a, what is lim[f(x)g(x)]?', '1', '12', '−7', '−12', 'd', 1, 'Use the product rule for limits.', 'lim(fg)=4(−3)=−12. Option A: added the limits. Option B: lost the negative sign. Option C: subtracted instead of multiplying.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0189', 7, 'If lim f(x)=5 and k=−3, what is lim[kf(x)]?', '15', '−2', '−15', '−8', 'c', 1, 'Multiply the constant by the limiting value.', 'lim[kf(x)]=k·lim f(x)=(−3)(5)=−15. Option A: dropped the negative sign. Option B: subtracted 3 from 5. Option D: added k to the limit.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0190', 7, 'If lim f(x)=12 and lim g(x)=4, what is lim[f(x)/g(x)]?', '48', '16', '8', '3', 'd', 1, 'Use the quotient rule, noting that the denominator limit is nonzero.', 'lim(f/g)=12/4=3. Option A: multiplied instead of divided. Option B: added instead of divided. Option C: subtracted instead of divided.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0191', 7, 'Using direct substitution, evaluate lim(x→3)(2x²−x+4).', '13', '21', '17', '19', 'd', 1, 'Substitute x=3 into every occurrence of x.', '2(3)²−3+4=18−3+4=19. Option A: forgot the square on x. Option B: added x instead of subtracting it. Option C: used 2·3−3+4.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0192', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→−2)(x³+2x²−x).', '-6', '−14', '2', '6', 'c', 2, 'Directly substitute −2 and keep powers and signs intact.', '(−2)³+2(−2)²−(−2)=−8+8+2=2. Option A: sign error in the cubic term. Option B: made sign errors in both the cubic and linear terms. Option D: used −2² as −4 before multiplying by 2.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0193', 7, 'Using the power theorem, evaluate lim(x→3)[(x³−27)/(x−3)].', '9', '27', '81', '3', 'b', 1, 'Apply n a^(n−1) with n=3 and a=3.', 'The theorem gives 3(3)²=27. Option A: used n·a instead of n·a². Option C: used a³. Option D: forgot the factor n.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0194', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→2)(x^(1/2)).', '4', '2', '√2', '1/√2', 'c', 1, 'Use the standard result lim(x→a) x^r = a^r.', 'Substituting x=2 gives 2^(1/2)=√2. Option A: squared the result. Option B: used x instead of √x. Option D: inverted the square root.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0195', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→5)(7).', '7', '0', '35', '5', 'a', 1, 'The limit of a constant is the same constant.', 'lim(x→5)7=7. Option B: treated a constant as zero. Option C: multiplied by the approach value. Option D: used the approach value instead of the function value.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0196', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→4)x.', '4', '16', '1', '0', 'a', 1, 'Use lim(x→a)x=a.', 'At x=4 the limiting value is 4. Option B: squared x unnecessarily. Option C: used a reciprocal. Option D: confused x with a constant zero.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0197', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→−2)x³.', '−4', '8', '−8', '−6', 'c', 1, 'Use lim x^n=a^n.', '(−2)³=−8. Option A: used x². Option B: lost the negative sign. Option D: used 3x.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0198', 7, 'If p(x)=3x²−2x+1, evaluate lim(x→2)p(x).', '7', '13', '5', '9', 'd', 1, 'A polynomial limit equals the polynomial evaluated at the approach value.', 'p(2)=3(4)−4+1=9. Option A: forgot the x² coefficient. Option B: used 3x²+2x+1. Option C: used x=1.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0199', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→3) [(x²−9)/(x−3)] using factorization.', '3', '0', '9', '6', 'd', 2, 'Factor x²−9 before cancelling x−3.', 'x²−9=(x−3)(x+3); cancel x−3 and substitute x=3 to get 6. Option A: cancelled x−3 and then used only one term. Option B: treated the cancelled factor as zero. Option C: substituted into the uncancelled form.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0200', 7, 'When rationalizing [√(5+x)−√(5−x)]/x by its conjugate, what is the numerator after multiplication?', '10', '2x', '−2x', 'x', 'b', 2, 'Multiply the numerator by √(5+x)+√(5−x) and use the difference of squares.', '(√(5+x))²−(√(5−x))²=(5+x)−(5−x)=2x. Option A: added the two constants instead of using a difference of squares. Option C: made a sign error in the difference of squares. Option D: forgot the second square-root term.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0201', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0)[(e^x−1)/x].', '1', '−1', 'e', '0', 'a', 1, 'Use the standard exponential result involving e^x−1.', 'The standard result gives lim(x→0)(e^x−1)/x=loge=1. Option B: made a sign error. Option C: used the base e itself. Option D: used the numerator limit alone.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0202', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0)[log(1+x)/x].', '0', 'e', '1', '−1', 'c', 1, 'Use the standard logarithmic result given for log(1+x)/x.', 'The standard result gives 1. Option A: used the numerator limit alone. Option B: confused log with its base. Option D: made a sign error.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0203', 7, 'For a>0, evaluate lim(x→0)[(a^x−1)/x] when a=9.', '9', 'log 9', '81', 'log(1/9)', 'b', 1, 'Apply the standard result with a=9.', 'The limit equals log 9. Option A: used the base instead of its logarithm. Option C: squared the base. Option D: reversed the ratio.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0204', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0)[(4^x−1)/x + (3^x−1)/x].', 'log 12', '12', 'log 7', 'log(4/3)', 'a', 2, 'Apply the exponential standard result to each term and then add.', 'The limit is log4+log3=log(4·3)=log12. Option B: removed the logarithm. Option C: added the bases before taking logarithms. Option D: subtracted the logarithms.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0205', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0)[(5^x−1)/x − (2^x−1)/x].', 'log3', 'log(5/2)', 'log10', 'log(2/5)', 'b', 2, 'Apply the standard exponential limit to both terms and subtract.', 'The result is log5−log2=log(5/2). Option A: subtracted the bases before taking logs. Option C: multiplied the bases. Option D: reversed the subtraction.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0206', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0)[log(1+6x)/x].', '1/6', '6', '36', '7', 'b', 1, 'Rewrite the denominator as 6x and use the standard log limit.', 'The expression equals 6·log(1+6x)/(6x), so the limit is 6. Option A: inverted the multiplier. Option C: used 6². Option D: added 1 to the coefficient.'),
        ('mth11a-07-mcq-0207', 7, 'Evaluate lim(x→0)[(9^x−1)(4^x−1)/x²].', '(log 9)/(log 4)', '(log 9)(log 4)', '(log 9)+(log 4)', 'log36', 'b', 2, 'Separate the product into two factors each containing x in the denominator.', 'The expression is [(9^x−1)/x][(4^x−1)/x], so the limit is (log9)(log4). Option A: divided the limits. Option C: added instead of multiplied. Option D: combined the logarithms.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0001', 8, 'Let f(x) = |x−3|. Find lim(x→3) f(x).', '−1', '1', '0', '2', 'c', 1, 'Evaluate the absolute-value expression as x approaches the specified point.', 'As x approaches 3, |x−3| approaches |3−3|=0, so the limit is 0. Option A (−1) comes from dropping the absolute-value sign and producing a negative value. Option B (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1. Option D (2) comes from using an unrelated nonzero value instead of the limiting expression.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0002', 8, 'A distance model is d(t)=|t−2|. What is lim(t→2) d(t)?', '0', '−1', '2', '1', 'a', 1, 'Interpret the absolute value as the distance from the marked point and evaluate at t=2.', 'As t approaches 2, |t−2| approaches |2−2|=0, so the limit is 0. Option B (−1) comes from a sign error. Option C (2) comes from using the point value instead of the zero distance. Option D (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0003', 8, 'A position model is p(t)=|t+1|. Find lim(t→−1) p(t).', '−1', '1', '0', '2', 'c', 1, 'Substitute the limiting position into the absolute-value expression.', 'As t approaches −1, |t+1| approaches |−1+1|=0, so the limit is 0. Option A (−1) comes from a sign error. Option B (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1. Option D (2) comes from using a nonzero value instead of the limiting expression.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0004', 8, 'Let f(x) = |x+4|. Find lim(x→-4) f(x).', '2', '−1', '1', '0', 'd', 1, 'Evaluate the absolute-value expression as x approaches the specified point.', 'As x approaches -4, |x−-4| approaches |-4−-4|=0, so the limit is 0. Option A (2) comes from using an unrelated nonzero value instead of the limiting expression. Option B (−1) comes from dropping the absolute-value sign and producing a negative value. Option C (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0005', 8, 'Let f(x) = |x−6|. Find lim(x→6) f(x).', '1', '−1', '0', '2', 'c', 1, 'Evaluate the absolute-value expression as x approaches the specified point.', 'As x approaches 6, |x−6| approaches |6−6|=0, so the limit is 0. Option A (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1. Option B (−1) comes from dropping the absolute-value sign and producing a negative value. Option D (2) comes from using an unrelated nonzero value instead of the limiting expression.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0006', 8, 'Let f(x) = |x−7|. Find lim(x→7) f(x).', '0', '1', '−1', '2', 'a', 1, 'Evaluate the absolute-value expression as x approaches the specified point.', 'As x approaches 7, |x−7| approaches |7−7|=0, so the limit is 0. Option B (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1. Option C (−1) comes from dropping the absolute-value sign and producing a negative value. Option D (2) comes from using an unrelated nonzero value instead of the limiting expression.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0007', 8, 'Let f(x) = |x+3|. Find lim(x→-3) f(x).', '−1', '2', '1', '0', 'd', 1, 'Evaluate the absolute-value expression as x approaches the specified point.', 'As x approaches -3, |x−-3| approaches |-3−-3|=0, so the limit is 0. Option A (−1) comes from dropping the absolute-value sign and producing a negative value. Option B (2) comes from using an unrelated nonzero value instead of the limiting expression. Option C (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0008', 8, 'Let f(x) = |x−8|. Find lim(x→8) f(x).', '−1', '0', '2', '1', 'b', 1, 'Evaluate the absolute-value expression as x approaches the specified point.', 'As x approaches 8, |x−8| approaches |8−8|=0, so the limit is 0. Option A (−1) comes from dropping the absolute-value sign and producing a negative value. Option C (2) comes from using an unrelated nonzero value instead of the limiting expression. Option D (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0009', 8, 'Let f(x) = |x−9|. Find lim(x→9) f(x).', '−1', '1', '2', '0', 'd', 1, 'Evaluate the absolute-value expression as x approaches the specified point.', 'As x approaches 9, |x−9| approaches |9−9|=0, so the limit is 0. Option A (−1) comes from dropping the absolute-value sign and producing a negative value. Option B (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1. Option C (2) comes from using an unrelated nonzero value instead of the limiting expression.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0010', 8, 'Let f(x) = |x+5|. Find lim(x→-5) f(x).', '2', '−1', '1', '0', 'd', 1, 'Evaluate the absolute-value expression as x approaches the specified point.', 'As x approaches -5, |x−-5| approaches |-5−-5|=0, so the limit is 0. Option A (2) comes from using an unrelated nonzero value instead of the limiting expression. Option B (−1) comes from dropping the absolute-value sign and producing a negative value. Option C (1) comes from replacing the zero difference by 1.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0011', 8, 'Let f(x)=x². Find lim(x→3) f(x).', '9', '11', '3', '-9', 'a', 1, 'A polynomial can be evaluated directly at the indicated point.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→3)x²=(3)²=9. Option B (11) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2. Option C (3) comes from forgetting to square the input. Option D (-9) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0012', 8, 'A height model is h(t)=t². What is lim(t→−2) h(t)?', '4', '-2', '6', '-4', 'a', 1, 'For this polynomial model, evaluate the square at the indicated time value.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→-2)x²=(-2)²=4. Option B (-2) comes from forgetting to square the input. Option C (6) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2. Option D (-4) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0013', 8, 'A square-area model is A(s)=s². Find lim(s→5) A(s).', '27', '-25', '25', '5', 'c', 1, 'Evaluate the polynomial expression at the approaching side length.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→5)x²=(5)²=25. Option A (27) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2. Option B (-25) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring. Option D (5) comes from forgetting to square the input.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0014', 8, 'Let f(x)=x². Find lim(x→-4) f(x).', '18', '-4', '16', '-16', 'c', 1, 'A polynomial can be evaluated directly at the indicated point.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→-4)x²=(-4)²=16. Option A (18) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2. Option B (-4) comes from forgetting to square the input. Option D (-16) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0015', 8, 'Let f(x)=x². Find lim(x→6) f(x).', '-36', '36', '38', '6', 'b', 1, 'A polynomial can be evaluated directly at the indicated point.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→6)x²=(6)²=36. Option A (-36) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring. Option C (38) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2. Option D (6) comes from forgetting to square the input.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0016', 8, 'Let f(x)=x². Find lim(x→7) f(x).', '7', '49', '51', '-49', 'b', 1, 'A polynomial can be evaluated directly at the indicated point.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→7)x²=(7)²=49. Option A (7) comes from forgetting to square the input. Option C (51) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2. Option D (-49) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0017', 8, 'Let f(x)=x². Find lim(x→-3) f(x).', '-3', '-9', '9', '11', 'c', 1, 'A polynomial can be evaluated directly at the indicated point.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→-3)x²=(-3)²=9. Option A (-3) comes from forgetting to square the input. Option B (-9) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring. Option D (11) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0018', 8, 'Let f(x)=x². Find lim(x→8) f(x).', '66', '64', '-64', '8', 'b', 1, 'A polynomial can be evaluated directly at the indicated point.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→8)x²=(8)²=64. Option A (66) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2. Option C (-64) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring. Option D (8) comes from forgetting to square the input.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0019', 8, 'Let f(x)=x². Find lim(x→9) f(x).', '81', '83', '-81', '9', 'a', 1, 'A polynomial can be evaluated directly at the indicated point.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→9)x²=(9)²=81. Option B (83) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2. Option C (-81) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring. Option D (9) comes from forgetting to square the input.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0020', 8, 'Let f(x)=x². Find lim(x→-5) f(x).', '-5', '-25', '25', '27', 'c', 1, 'A polynomial can be evaluated directly at the indicated point.', 'Direct evaluation gives lim(x→-5)x²=(-5)²=25. Option A (-5) comes from forgetting to square the input. Option B (-25) comes from retaining an incorrect negative sign after squaring. Option D (27) comes from making the arithmetic error of adding 2.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0021', 8, 'For f(x)=[x], what are the left-hand and right-hand limits at x=4?', '3 and 4', '3 and 3', '4 and 3', '4 and 4', 'a', 2, 'Use the greatest integer immediately below the approaching value on the left and the value reached on the right.', 'For x→4−, [x]=3, while for x→4+, [x]=4. Thus LHL=3 and RHL=4. Option B (3 and 3) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides. Option C (4 and 3) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values. Option D (4 and 4) comes from using the point value on both sides.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0022', 8, 'For f(x)=[x], what are the left-hand and right-hand limits at x=5?', '4 and 4', '5 and 5', '5 and 4', '4 and 5', 'd', 2, 'Use the greatest integer immediately below the approaching value on the left and the value reached on the right.', 'For x→5−, [x]=4, while for x→5+, [x]=5. Thus LHL=4 and RHL=5. Option A (4 and 4) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides. Option B (5 and 5) comes from using the point value on both sides. Option C (5 and 4) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0023', 8, 'A step-count model is C(x)=[x], where [x] is the greatest integer function. At x=6, what are the left-hand and right-hand limits?', '5 and 6', '6 and 6', '5 and 5', '6 and 5', 'a', 2, 'For the floor function, inspect values just below and just above the integer 6.', 'For x→6−, [x]=5, while for x→6+, [x]=6. Thus LHL=5 and RHL=6. Option B (6 and 6) comes from using the point value on both sides. Option C (5 and 5) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides. Option D (6 and 5) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0024', 8, 'A billing model charges B(x)=[x], with [x] the greatest integer function. At x=7, what are the left-hand and right-hand limits?', '7 and 7', '7 and 6', '6 and 7', '6 and 6', 'c', 2, 'Use the greatest integer immediately below values just to the left and the integer reached just to the right.', 'For x→7−, [x]=6, while for x→7+, [x]=7. Thus LHL=6 and RHL=7. Option A (7 and 7) comes from using the point value on both sides. Option B (7 and 6) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values. Option D (6 and 6) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0025', 8, 'For f(x)=[x], what are the left-hand and right-hand limits at x=8?', '8 and 7', '7 and 7', '8 and 8', '7 and 8', 'd', 2, 'Use the greatest integer immediately below the approaching value on the left and the value reached on the right.', 'For x→8−, [x]=7, while for x→8+, [x]=8. Thus LHL=7 and RHL=8. Option A (8 and 7) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values. Option B (7 and 7) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides. Option C (8 and 8) comes from using the point value on both sides.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0026', 8, 'For f(x)=[x], what are the left-hand and right-hand limits at x=9?', '8 and 9', '9 and 8', '8 and 8', '9 and 9', 'a', 2, 'Use the greatest integer immediately below the approaching value on the left and the value reached on the right.', 'For x→9−, [x]=8, while for x→9+, [x]=9. Thus LHL=8 and RHL=9. Option B (9 and 8) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values. Option C (8 and 8) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides. Option D (9 and 9) comes from using the point value on both sides.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0027', 8, 'For f(x)=[x], what are the left-hand and right-hand limits at x=10?', '10 and 10', '9 and 10', '10 and 9', '9 and 9', 'b', 2, 'Use the greatest integer immediately below the approaching value on the left and the value reached on the right.', 'For x→10−, [x]=9, while for x→10+, [x]=10. Thus LHL=9 and RHL=10. Option A (10 and 10) comes from using the point value on both sides. Option C (10 and 9) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values. Option D (9 and 9) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0028', 8, 'For f(x)=[x], what are the left-hand and right-hand limits at x=11?', '10 and 11', '11 and 11', '10 and 10', '11 and 10', 'a', 2, 'Use the greatest integer immediately below the approaching value on the left and the value reached on the right.', 'For x→11−, [x]=10, while for x→11+, [x]=11. Thus LHL=10 and RHL=11. Option B (11 and 11) comes from using the point value on both sides. Option C (10 and 10) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides. Option D (11 and 10) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0029', 8, 'For f(x)=[x], what are the left-hand and right-hand limits at x=12?', '11 and 12', '11 and 11', '12 and 12', '12 and 11', 'a', 2, 'Use the greatest integer immediately below the approaching value on the left and the value reached on the right.', 'For x→12−, [x]=11, while for x→12+, [x]=12. Thus LHL=11 and RHL=12. Option B (11 and 11) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides. Option C (12 and 12) comes from using the point value on both sides. Option D (12 and 11) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0030', 8, 'For f(x)=[x], what are the left-hand and right-hand limits at x=13?', '12 and 13', '12 and 12', '13 and 12', '13 and 13', 'a', 2, 'Use the greatest integer immediately below the approaching value on the left and the value reached on the right.', 'For x→13−, [x]=12, while for x→13+, [x]=13. Thus LHL=12 and RHL=13. Option B (12 and 12) comes from using the left-hand value on both sides. Option C (13 and 12) comes from reversing the left- and right-hand values. Option D (13 and 13) comes from using the point value on both sides.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0031', 8, 'For f(x)=x²+(1) for x≤2 and f(x)=3x+(-1) for x>2, find (LHL,RHL) at x=2.', '(6,4)', '(4,5)', '(5,6)', '(5,5)', 'd', 2, 'Evaluate the first branch from the left and the second branch from the right at the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 5 and the right-hand limit is 5, so (LHL,RHL)=(5,5). Option A ((6,4)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option B ((4,5)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option C ((5,6)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0032', 8, 'For a cost model C(x)=x²+2 for x≤3 and C(x)=4x−10 for x>3, find (LHL,RHL) at x=3.', '(2,11)', '(12,2)', '(11,2)', '(11,3)', 'c', 2, 'Evaluate the cost formula from each side of the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 11 and the right-hand limit is 2, so (LHL,RHL)=(11,2). Option A ((2,11)) comes from swapping the one-sided limits. Option B ((12,2)) comes from making an arithmetic error in the left-hand limit. Option D ((11,3)) comes from making an arithmetic error in the right-hand limit.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0033', 8, 'For a temperature model T(x)=x²+3 for x≤4 and T(x)=5x−17 for x>4, find (LHL,RHL) at x=4.', '(19,3)', '(3,19)', '(19,4)', '(20,3)', 'a', 2, 'Evaluate T from the left and right at the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 19 and the right-hand limit is 3, so (LHL,RHL)=(19,3). Option B ((3,19)) comes from swapping the one-sided limits. Option C ((19,4)) comes from making an arithmetic error in the right-hand limit. Option D ((20,3)) comes from making an arithmetic error in the left-hand limit.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0034', 8, 'For f(x)=x²+(1) for x≤5 and f(x)=6x+(-4) for x>5, find (LHL,RHL) at x=5.', '(25,26)', '(27,25)', '(26,26)', '(26,27)', 'c', 2, 'Evaluate the first branch from the left and the second branch from the right at the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 26 and the right-hand limit is 26, so (LHL,RHL)=(26,26). Option A ((25,26)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option B ((27,25)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option D ((26,27)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0035', 8, 'For f(x)=x²+(-2) for x≤6 and f(x)=4x+(10) for x>6, find (LHL,RHL) at x=6.', '(34,35)', '(33,34)', '(35,33)', '(34,34)', 'd', 2, 'Evaluate the first branch from the left and the second branch from the right at the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 34 and the right-hand limit is 34, so (LHL,RHL)=(34,34). Option A ((34,35)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option B ((33,34)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option C ((35,33)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0036', 8, 'For f(x)=x²+(3) for x≤7 and f(x)=5x+(-22) for x>7, find (LHL,RHL) at x=7.', '(13,52)', '(53,13)', '(52,14)', '(52,13)', 'd', 2, 'Evaluate the first branch from the left and the second branch from the right at the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 52 and the right-hand limit is 13, so (LHL,RHL)=(52,13). Option A ((13,52)) comes from swapping the left- and right-hand limits. Option B ((53,13)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option C ((52,14)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0037', 8, 'For f(x)=x²+(0) for x≤8 and f(x)=2x+(-12) for x>8, find (LHL,RHL) at x=8.', '(64,5)', '(64,4)', '(4,64)', '(65,4)', 'b', 2, 'Evaluate the first branch from the left and the second branch from the right at the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 64 and the right-hand limit is 4, so (LHL,RHL)=(64,4). Option A ((64,5)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option C ((4,64)) comes from swapping the left- and right-hand limits. Option D ((65,4)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0038', 8, 'For f(x)=x²+(4) for x≤9 and f(x)=7x+(-25) for x>9, find (LHL,RHL) at x=9.', '(86,38)', '(38,85)', '(85,38)', '(85,39)', 'c', 2, 'Evaluate the first branch from the left and the second branch from the right at the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 85 and the right-hand limit is 38, so (LHL,RHL)=(85,38). Option A ((86,38)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option B ((38,85)) comes from swapping the left- and right-hand limits. Option D ((85,39)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0039', 8, 'For f(x)=x²+(-3) for x≤10 and f(x)=11x+(-99) for x>10, find (LHL,RHL) at x=10.', '(97,12)', '(11,97)', '(97,11)', '(98,11)', 'c', 2, 'Evaluate the first branch from the left and the second branch from the right at the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 97 and the right-hand limit is 11, so (LHL,RHL)=(97,11). Option A ((97,12)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option B ((11,97)) comes from swapping the left- and right-hand limits. Option D ((98,11)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0040', 8, 'For f(x)=x²+(2) for x≤11 and f(x)=8x+(-10) for x>11, find (LHL,RHL) at x=11.', '(124,78)', '(123,78)', '(78,123)', '(123,79)', 'b', 2, 'Evaluate the first branch from the left and the second branch from the right at the joining point.', 'The left-hand limit is 123 and the right-hand limit is 78, so (LHL,RHL)=(123,78). Option A ((124,78)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits. Option C ((78,123)) comes from swapping the left- and right-hand limits. Option D ((123,79)) comes from making an arithmetic error in one of the one-sided limits.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0041', 8, 'Let f(x)=3x+1 for x<2, f(x)=7 for 2≤x<4, and f(x)=x²+(-6) for x≥4. At which joining point is f discontinuous?', 'x=3', 'x=4', 'x=5', 'x=2', 'b', 3, 'Check continuity only at the points where the formula changes, using both one-sided limits.', 'At x=2, the left limit and middle value are both 7, so continuity holds. At x=4, the left limit is 7, while the right limit and f(4) are 10; these differ, so discontinuity occurs at x=4. Option A (x=3) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point. Option C (x=5) comes from choosing a point outside the two joining points. Option D (x=2) comes from choosing the first join even though its one-sided limits agree.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0042', 8, 'A three-stage tariff is f(x)=3x+1 for x<4, f(x)=13 for 4≤x<7, and f(x)=x²−12 for x≥7. At which joining point is the tariff discontinuous?', 'x=5', 'x=4', 'x=8', 'x=7', 'd', 3, 'At each formula change, compare the left and right limits and the actual value.', 'At x=4, the left limit 3(4)+1=13 equals the middle value 13, so continuity holds. At x=7, the left limit is 13, while the right limit and f(7) are 7²−12=37, so discontinuity occurs at x=7. Option A (x=5) comes from choosing a non-joining point. Option B (x=4) comes from choosing the continuous first join. Option C (x=8) comes from choosing a non-joining point.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0043', 8, 'A production cost function is f(x)=3x+1 for x<5, f(x)=16 for 5≤x<8, and f(x)=x²−10 for x≥8. At which joining point is f discontinuous?', 'x=5', 'x=9', 'x=6', 'x=8', 'd', 3, 'Check the two points where the formula changes: x=5 and x=8.', 'At x=5, the left limit 3(5)+1=16 equals the middle value 16, so continuity holds. At x=8, the left limit is 16, while the right limit and f(8) are 8²−10=54, so discontinuity occurs at x=8. Option A (x=5) comes from choosing the continuous first join. Option B (x=9) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point. Option C (x=6) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0044', 8, 'Let f(x)=3x+1 for x<5, f(x)=16 for 5≤x<8, and f(x)=x²+(-10) for x≥8. At which joining point is f discontinuous?', 'x=5', 'x=6', 'x=8', 'x=9', 'c', 3, 'Check continuity only at the points where the formula changes, using both one-sided limits.', 'At x=5, the left limit and middle value are both 16, so continuity holds. At x=8, the left limit is 16, while the right limit and f(8) are 54; these differ, so discontinuity occurs at x=8. Option A (x=5) comes from choosing the first join even though its one-sided limits agree. Option B (x=6) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point. Option D (x=9) comes from choosing a point outside the two joining points.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0045', 8, 'Let f(x)=3x+1 for x<6, f(x)=19 for 6≤x<10, and f(x)=x²+(-9) for x≥10. At which joining point is f discontinuous?', 'x=6', 'x=10', 'x=7', 'x=11', 'b', 3, 'Check continuity only at the points where the formula changes, using both one-sided limits.', 'At x=6, the left limit and middle value are both 19, so continuity holds. At x=10, the left limit is 19, while the right limit and f(10) are 91; these differ, so discontinuity occurs at x=10. Option A (x=6) comes from choosing the first join even though its one-sided limits agree. Option C (x=7) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point. Option D (x=11) comes from choosing a point outside the two joining points.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0046', 8, 'Let f(x)=3x+1 for x<7, f(x)=22 for 7≤x<12, and f(x)=x²+(-8) for x≥12. At which joining point is f discontinuous?', 'x=13', 'x=12', 'x=8', 'x=7', 'b', 3, 'Check continuity only at the points where the formula changes, using both one-sided limits.', 'At x=7, the left limit and middle value are both 22, so continuity holds. At x=12, the left limit is 22, while the right limit and f(12) are 136; these differ, so discontinuity occurs at x=12. Option A (x=13) comes from choosing a point outside the two joining points. Option C (x=8) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point. Option D (x=7) comes from choosing the first join even though its one-sided limits agree.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0047', 8, 'Let f(x)=3x+1 for x<8, f(x)=25 for 8≤x<14, and f(x)=x²+(-6) for x≥14. At which joining point is f discontinuous?', 'x=8', 'x=15', 'x=14', 'x=9', 'c', 3, 'Check continuity only at the points where the formula changes, using both one-sided limits.', 'At x=8, the left limit and middle value are both 25, so continuity holds. At x=14, the left limit is 25, while the right limit and f(14) are 190; these differ, so discontinuity occurs at x=14. Option A (x=8) comes from choosing the first join even though its one-sided limits agree. Option B (x=15) comes from choosing a point outside the two joining points. Option D (x=9) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0048', 8, 'Let f(x)=3x+1 for x<9, f(x)=28 for 9≤x<16, and f(x)=x²+(-5) for x≥16. At which joining point is f discontinuous?', 'x=10', 'x=9', 'x=17', 'x=16', 'd', 3, 'Check continuity only at the points where the formula changes, using both one-sided limits.', 'At x=9, the left limit and middle value are both 28, so continuity holds. At x=16, the left limit is 28, while the right limit and f(16) are 251; these differ, so discontinuity occurs at x=16. Option A (x=10) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point. Option B (x=9) comes from choosing the first join even though its one-sided limits agree. Option C (x=17) comes from choosing a point outside the two joining points.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0049', 8, 'Let f(x)=3x+1 for x<10, f(x)=31 for 10≤x<18, and f(x)=x²+(-4) for x≥18. At which joining point is f discontinuous?', 'x=11', 'x=18', 'x=10', 'x=19', 'b', 3, 'Check continuity only at the points where the formula changes, using both one-sided limits.', 'At x=10, the left limit and middle value are both 31, so continuity holds. At x=18, the left limit is 31, while the right limit and f(18) are 320; these differ, so discontinuity occurs at x=18. Option A (x=11) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point. Option C (x=10) comes from choosing the first join even though its one-sided limits agree. Option D (x=19) comes from choosing a point outside the two joining points.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0050', 8, 'Let f(x)=3x+1 for x<11, f(x)=34 for 11≤x<20, and f(x)=x²+(-3) for x≥20. At which joining point is f discontinuous?', 'x=21', 'x=12', 'x=11', 'x=20', 'd', 3, 'Check continuity only at the points where the formula changes, using both one-sided limits.', 'At x=11, the left limit and middle value are both 34, so continuity holds. At x=20, the left limit is 34, while the right limit and f(20) are 397; these differ, so discontinuity occurs at x=20. Option A (x=21) comes from choosing a point outside the two joining points. Option B (x=12) comes from choosing a point that is not a joining point. Option C (x=11) comes from choosing the first join even though its one-sided limits agree.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0051', 8, 'For x≠-4, let f(x)=(x²+16x+48)/(x+4). Find lim(x→-4) f(x).', '9', '8', '4', '-8', 'b', 2, 'Factor the numerator, cancel the common factor for x≠the excluded point, then evaluate the remaining expression at the limit point.', 'x²+16x+48=(x+4)(x+12), so f(x)=x+12 for x≠-4. Hence lim(x→-4)f(x)=8. Option A (9) comes from making a one-unit arithmetic error. Option C (4) comes from using the cancelled factor instead of the remaining limit value. Option D (-8) comes from introducing a sign error.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0052', 8, 'An efficiency model is f(x)=(x²+19x+84)/(x+6) for x≠−6. Find lim(x→−6)f(x).', '9', '−8', '6', '8', 'd', 2, 'Factor x²+19x+84 and cancel the common factor x+6.', 'x²+19x+84=(x+6)(x+14), so the quotient becomes x+14 for x≠−6. Therefore the limit is −6+14=8. Option A (9) comes from adding 1 to the correct value. Option B (−8) comes from introducing a sign error. Option C (6) comes from using 6 instead of the remaining value 8.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0053', 8, 'A ratio model is f(x)=(x²+31x+210)/(x+14) for x≠−14. Find lim(x→−14)f(x).', '−1', '1', '14', '2', 'b', 2, 'Factor the numerator into two linear factors and cancel x+14.', 'x²+31x+210=(x+14)(x+15), so f(x)=x+15 for x≠−14. Therefore the limit is −14+15=1. Option A (−1) comes from introducing a sign error. Option C (14) comes from using 14 instead of the remaining value 1. Option D (2) comes from adding 1 to the correct value.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0054', 8, 'For x≠-7, let f(x)=(x²+27x+140)/(x+7). Find lim(x→-7) f(x).', '14', '7', '13', '-13', 'c', 2, 'Factor the numerator, cancel the common factor for x≠the excluded point, then evaluate the remaining expression at the limit point.', 'x²+27x+140=(x+7)(x+20), so f(x)=x+20 for x≠-7. Hence lim(x→-7)f(x)=13. Option A (14) comes from making a one-unit arithmetic error. Option B (7) comes from using the cancelled factor instead of the remaining limit value. Option D (-13) comes from introducing a sign error.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0055', 8, 'For x≠-8, let f(x)=(x²+31x+184)/(x+8). Find lim(x→-8) f(x).', '-15', '8', '16', '15', 'd', 2, 'Factor the numerator, cancel the common factor for x≠the excluded point, then evaluate the remaining expression at the limit point.', 'x²+31x+184=(x+8)(x+23), so f(x)=x+23 for x≠-8. Hence lim(x→-8)f(x)=15. Option A (-15) comes from introducing a sign error. Option B (8) comes from using the cancelled factor instead of the remaining limit value. Option C (16) comes from making a one-unit arithmetic error.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0056', 8, 'For x≠-9, let f(x)=(x²+35x+234)/(x+9). Find lim(x→-9) f(x).', '18', '-17', '17', '9', 'c', 2, 'Factor the numerator, cancel the common factor for x≠the excluded point, then evaluate the remaining expression at the limit point.', 'x²+35x+234=(x+9)(x+26), so f(x)=x+26 for x≠-9. Hence lim(x→-9)f(x)=17. Option A (18) comes from making a one-unit arithmetic error. Option B (-17) comes from introducing a sign error. Option D (9) comes from using the cancelled factor instead of the remaining limit value.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0057', 8, 'For x≠-10, let f(x)=(x²+39x+290)/(x+10). Find lim(x→-10) f(x).', '20', '10', '-19', '19', 'd', 2, 'Factor the numerator, cancel the common factor for x≠the excluded point, then evaluate the remaining expression at the limit point.', 'x²+39x+290=(x+10)(x+29), so f(x)=x+29 for x≠-10. Hence lim(x→-10)f(x)=19. Option A (20) comes from making a one-unit arithmetic error. Option B (10) comes from using the cancelled factor instead of the remaining limit value. Option C (-19) comes from introducing a sign error.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0058', 8, 'For x≠-11, let f(x)=(x²+43x+352)/(x+11). Find lim(x→-11) f(x).', '22', '-21', '11', '21', 'd', 2, 'Factor the numerator, cancel the common factor for x≠the excluded point, then evaluate the remaining expression at the limit point.', 'x²+43x+352=(x+11)(x+32), so f(x)=x+32 for x≠-11. Hence lim(x→-11)f(x)=21. Option A (22) comes from making a one-unit arithmetic error. Option B (-21) comes from introducing a sign error. Option C (11) comes from using the cancelled factor instead of the remaining limit value.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0059', 8, 'For x≠-12, let f(x)=(x²+47x+420)/(x+12). Find lim(x→-12) f(x).', '24', '12', '-23', '23', 'd', 2, 'Factor the numerator, cancel the common factor for x≠the excluded point, then evaluate the remaining expression at the limit point.', 'x²+47x+420=(x+12)(x+35), so f(x)=x+35 for x≠-12. Hence lim(x→-12)f(x)=23. Option A (24) comes from making a one-unit arithmetic error. Option B (12) comes from using the cancelled factor instead of the remaining limit value. Option C (-23) comes from introducing a sign error.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0060', 8, 'For x≠-13, let f(x)=(x²+51x+494)/(x+13). Find lim(x→-13) f(x).', '13', '25', '26', '-25', 'b', 2, 'Factor the numerator, cancel the common factor for x≠the excluded point, then evaluate the remaining expression at the limit point.', 'x²+51x+494=(x+13)(x+38), so f(x)=x+38 for x≠-13. Hence lim(x→-13)f(x)=25. Option A (13) comes from using the cancelled factor instead of the remaining limit value. Option C (26) comes from making a one-unit arithmetic error. Option D (-25) comes from introducing a sign error.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0061', 8, 'Suppose f(x)=(( 2x+2 )/(2−4x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If f is continuous at x=0, what is f(0)?', 'e^2', 'e^1', 'e^3', 'e^-3', 'c', 3, 'Rewrite each factor in the form (1+kx)^(1/x) and use the standard exponential limit.', 'The ratio is (1+2x/2)/(1−4x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(2/2)e^(4/2)=e^3, so f(0)=e^3. Option A (e^2) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution. Option B (e^1) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option D (e^-3) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0062', 8, 'A growth multiplier is M(x)=((2x+2)/(2−4x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If M is continuous at x=0, what is M(0)?', 'e^2', 'e^3', 'e^1', 'e^-3', 'b', 3, 'Rewrite the ratio as (1+x)/(1−2x) and separate the two standard exponential-limit forms.', 'The ratio is (1+4x/2)/(1−2x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(4/2)e^(2/2)=e^3, so f(0)=e^3. Option A (e^2) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option C (e^1) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution. Option D (e^-3) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0063', 8, 'A scaling factor is S(x)=((4x+2)/(2−2x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If S is continuous at x=0, what is S(0)?', 'e^-4', 'e^3', 'e^1', 'e^4', 'd', 3, 'Put the numerator and denominator into 2(1+kx) form and apply the standard exponential limit to each factor.', 'The ratio is (1+6x/2)/(1−2x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(6/2)e^(2/2)=e^4, so f(0)=e^4. Option A (e^-4) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors. Option B (e^3) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option C (e^1) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0064', 8, 'Suppose f(x)=(( 2x+2 )/(2−6x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If f is continuous at x=0, what is f(0)?', 'e^1', 'e^3', 'e^-4', 'e^4', 'd', 3, 'Rewrite each factor in the form (1+kx)^(1/x) and use the standard exponential limit.', 'The ratio is (1+2x/2)/(1−6x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(2/2)e^(6/2)=e^4, so f(0)=e^4. Option A (e^1) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option B (e^3) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution. Option C (e^-4) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0065', 8, 'Suppose f(x)=(( 8x+2 )/(2−4x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If f is continuous at x=0, what is f(0)?', 'e^-6', 'e^6', 'e^4', 'e^2', 'b', 3, 'Rewrite each factor in the form (1+kx)^(1/x) and use the standard exponential limit.', 'The ratio is (1+8x/2)/(1−4x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(8/2)e^(4/2)=e^6, so f(0)=e^6. Option A (e^-6) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors. Option C (e^4) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option D (e^2) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0066', 8, 'Suppose f(x)=(( 4x+2 )/(2−6x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If f is continuous at x=0, what is f(0)?', 'e^2', 'e^5', 'e^-5', 'e^3', 'b', 3, 'Rewrite each factor in the form (1+kx)^(1/x) and use the standard exponential limit.', 'The ratio is (1+4x/2)/(1−6x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(4/2)e^(6/2)=e^5, so f(0)=e^5. Option A (e^2) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option C (e^-5) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors. Option D (e^3) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0067', 8, 'Suppose f(x)=(( 10x+2 )/(2−2x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If f is continuous at x=0, what is f(0)?', 'e^5', 'e^6', 'e^-6', 'e^1', 'b', 3, 'Rewrite each factor in the form (1+kx)^(1/x) and use the standard exponential limit.', 'The ratio is (1+10x/2)/(1−2x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(10/2)e^(2/2)=e^6, so f(0)=e^6. Option A (e^5) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option C (e^-6) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors. Option D (e^1) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0068', 8, 'Suppose f(x)=(( 6x+2 )/(2−8x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If f is continuous at x=0, what is f(0)?', 'e^7', 'e^-7', 'e^3', 'e^4', 'a', 3, 'Rewrite each factor in the form (1+kx)^(1/x) and use the standard exponential limit.', 'The ratio is (1+6x/2)/(1−8x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(6/2)e^(8/2)=e^7, so f(0)=e^7. Option B (e^-7) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors. Option C (e^3) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option D (e^4) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0069', 8, 'Suppose f(x)=(( 12x+2 )/(2−4x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If f is continuous at x=0, what is f(0)?', 'e^-8', 'e^8', 'e^6', 'e^2', 'b', 3, 'Rewrite each factor in the form (1+kx)^(1/x) and use the standard exponential limit.', 'The ratio is (1+12x/2)/(1−4x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(12/2)e^(4/2)=e^8, so f(0)=e^8. Option A (e^-8) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors. Option C (e^6) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option D (e^2) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0070', 8, 'Suppose f(x)=(( 8x+2 )/(2−10x))^(1/x) for x≠0. If f is continuous at x=0, what is f(0)?', 'e^9', 'e^5', 'e^4', 'e^-9', 'a', 3, 'Rewrite each factor in the form (1+kx)^(1/x) and use the standard exponential limit.', 'The ratio is (1+8x/2)/(1−10x/2). Applying the standard exponential-limit form gives e^(8/2)e^(10/2)=e^9, so f(0)=e^9. Option B (e^5) comes from keeping only the denominator contribution. Option C (e^4) comes from keeping only the numerator contribution. Option D (e^-9) comes from miscombining the two exponential factors.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0071', 8, 'For continuity of f at x=a, which condition must hold?', 'f(a)∈R', 'f(a)=0', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=1', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=0', 'a', 1, 'Check whether the function has a defined value at the point.', 'The first continuity requirement is that f(a) is defined. Option B (f(a)=0) results from incorrectly forcing f(a) to be zero. Option C (lim(x→a)f(x)=1) results from assigning an unrelated limit value. Option D (lim(x→a)f(x)=0) results from incorrectly forcing the limit to be zero.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0072', 8, 'Which equality is required for lim(x→a)f(x) to exist?', 'lim(x→a−)f(x)=lim(x→a+)f(x)', 'f(a)=0', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=0', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=1', 'a', 1, 'Compare the two one-sided limits.', 'The two-sided limit exists when the left-hand and right-hand limits are equal. Option B (f(a)=0) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label. Option C (lim(x→a)f(x)=0) results from incorrectly requiring a limit to be zero. Option D (lim(x→a)f(x)=1) results from substituting an unrelated limit value.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0073', 8, 'Which equality is required after the limit exists for continuity at x=a?', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a)', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=0', 'f(a)=a', 'lim(x→a−)f(x)=0', 'a', 1, 'Compare the limit with the actual function value.', 'The third continuity condition is lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a). Option B (lim(x→a)f(x)=0) results from incorrectly requiring a limit to be zero. Option C (f(a)=a) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label. Option D (lim(x→a−)f(x)=0) results from incorrectly requiring a limit to be zero.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0074', 8, 'Which is the equivalent h-form continuity condition at x=a?', 'lim(h→0)[f(a+h)−f(a)]=0', 'lim(h→0)f(a+h)=0', 'lim(h→a)[f(a+h)−f(a)]=0', 'f(a+h)=0', 'a', 2, 'Use the reformulation involving an increment h tending to zero.', 'Continuity at x=a can be restated as lim(h→0)[f(a+h)−f(a)]=0. Option B (lim(h→0)f(a+h)=0) results from incorrectly requiring a limit to be zero. Option C (lim(h→a)[f(a+h)−f(a)]=0) results from incorrectly requiring a limit to be zero. Option D (f(a+h)=0) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0075', 8, 'If f is discontinuous at x=5, which x-value is the point of discontinuity?', '5', '0', '−5', '1', 'a', 1, 'The point of discontinuity is the x-coordinate where continuity fails.', 'The specified point is x=5, so the point of discontinuity has coordinate 5. Option B (0) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C (−5) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (1) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0076', 8, 'Which equality defines continuity from the right at x=a?', 'lim(x→a+)f(x)=f(a)', 'lim(x→a−)f(x)=f(a)', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=0', 'f(a)=a', 'a', 1, 'Approach a from values greater than a.', 'Right continuity requires lim(x→a+)f(x)=f(a). Option B (lim(x→a−)f(x)=f(a)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C (lim(x→a)f(x)=0) results from incorrectly requiring a limit to be zero. Option D (f(a)=a) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0077', 8, 'Which equality defines continuity from the left at x=b?', 'lim(x→b−)f(x)=f(b)', 'lim(x→b+)f(x)=f(b)', 'lim(x→b)f(x)=0', 'f(b)=b', 'a', 1, 'Approach b from values less than b.', 'Left continuity requires lim(x→b−)f(x)=f(b). Option B (lim(x→b+)f(x)=f(b)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C (lim(x→b)f(x)=0) results from incorrectly requiring a limit to be zero. Option D (f(b)=b) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0078', 8, 'If f and g are continuous at x=a, which expression is guaranteed continuous without a denominator restriction?', '(f+g)(x)', 'f(x)/g(x), g(a)=0', '1/f(x), f(a)=0', 'f(x)−g(x) with g(a)=0', 'a', 1, 'Use the sum property for continuous functions.', 'The sum (f+g)(x) is continuous when f and g are continuous. The other expressions introduce a zero-denominator or unnecessary restriction. Option B (f(x)/g(x), g(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter. Option C (1/f(x), f(a)=0) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label. Option D (f(x)−g(x) with g(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0079', 8, 'If f and g are continuous at x=a, which expression is guaranteed continuous as a difference?', 'f(x)g(x), f(a)=0', '(f−g)(x)', 'f(x)/g(x), g(a)=0', '(f+g)(x), g(a)=0', 'b', 1, 'Use the subtraction property.', 'The difference (f−g)(x) is continuous when f and g are continuous. Option A (f(x)g(x), f(a)=0) results from adding an unnecessary zero condition to a product or sum. Option C (f(x)/g(x), g(a)=0) results from using a quotient with zero denominator. Option D ((f+g)(x), g(a)=0) results from adding an unnecessary zero condition to a product or sum.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0080', 8, 'If f is continuous at x=a and k∈R, which expression is guaranteed continuous?', 'k f(x)', 'f(x)/k, k=0', 'k/f(x), f(a)=0', 'f(x)^k, f(a)=0', 'a', 1, 'Use multiplication by a real constant.', 'A constant multiple kf is continuous for any real k. Option B (f(x)/k, k=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter. Option C (k/f(x), f(a)=0) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label. Option D (f(x)^k, f(a)=0) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0081', 8, 'If f and g are continuous at x=a, which product is guaranteed continuous?', 'f(x)g(x)', 'f(x)/g(x), g(a)=0', 'f(x)/g(x), f(a)=0', 'f(x)+g(x), g(a)=0', 'a', 1, 'Use the product property.', 'The product f(x)g(x) is continuous when both factors are continuous. Option B (f(x)/g(x), g(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter. Option C (f(x)/g(x), f(a)=0) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label. Option D (f(x)+g(x), g(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0082', 8, 'For continuous f and g, which quotient condition guarantees continuity at x=a?', 'g(a)≠0', 'g(a)=0', 'f(a)=0', 'f(a)=g(a)=0', 'a', 2, 'Check the denominator at the point.', 'The quotient f/g is continuous at x=a when g(a)≠0. Option B (g(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter. Option C (f(a)=0) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label. Option D (f(a)=g(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0083', 8, 'Which composite expression is covered by the continuity property for composition?', 'f(g(x))', 'f(x)/g(x), g(a)=0', 'f(g(x)), g(a)=0', 'g(f(x)), f(a)=0', 'a', 2, 'Use the composition form f[g(x)].', 'The composite f(g(x)) is continuous when the relevant functions are continuous. The other choices impose zero-denominator or unnecessary zero conditions. Option B (f(x)/g(x), g(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter. Option C (f(g(x)), g(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter. Option D (g(f(x)), f(a)=0) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0084', 8, 'Which symbolic statement represents continuity throughout the open interval (a,b)?', '∀x∈(a,b): lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x)', 'f(a)=f(b)', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=lim(x→b)f(x)', '∃x∈(a,b): lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x)', 'a', 1, 'Express pointwise continuity using a universal condition over the open interval.', 'Continuity on (a,b) means the pointwise continuity equality holds for every x in (a,b). Option B (f(a)=f(b)) results from using endpoint equality instead of pointwise continuity. Option C (lim(x→a)f(x)=lim(x→b)f(x)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (∃x∈(a,b): lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x)) results from requiring continuity at only one point instead of every point.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0085', 8, 'Which endpoint condition is required for continuity on [a,b)?', 'lim(x→a+)f(x)=f(a)', 'lim(x→b−)f(x)=f(b)', 'lim(x→a−)f(x)=f(a)', 'lim(x→b+)f(x)=f(b)', 'a', 2, 'The included endpoint is a, approached from inside the interval.', 'For [a,b), f must be continuous on (a,b) and continuous from the right at a. Option B (lim(x→b−)f(x)=f(b)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C (lim(x→a−)f(x)=f(a)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (lim(x→b+)f(x)=f(b)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0086', 8, 'Which endpoint condition is required for continuity on (a,b]?', 'lim(x→b−)f(x)=f(b)', 'lim(x→a+)f(x)=f(a)', 'lim(x→b+)f(x)=f(b)', 'lim(x→a−)f(x)=f(a)', 'a', 2, 'The included endpoint is b, approached from inside the interval.', 'For (a,b], f must be continuous on (a,b) and continuous from the left at b. Option B (lim(x→a+)f(x)=f(a)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C (lim(x→b+)f(x)=f(b)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (lim(x→a−)f(x)=f(a)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0087', 8, 'If f is continuous on (a,b) and both endpoint limits exist, which closed interval can be used for a continuous extension?', '[a,b]', '(a,b)', '[a,b)', '(a,b]', 'a', 2, 'Include both endpoints using the existing endpoint limits.', 'When both endpoint limits exist, f can be extended to [a,b] continuously. Option B ((a,b)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C ([a,b)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D ((a,b]) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0088', 8, 'Which symbolic statement represents continuity at every point of an interval I?', '∀x∈I: lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x)', '∃x∈I: lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x)', 'f(a)=f(b)', '∀x∈I: f(x)=0', 'a', 1, 'Use a universal condition over every point of the interval.', 'A function is continuous in an interval when the continuity condition holds at every point of the interval. Option B (∃x∈I: lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x)) results from requiring continuity at only one point instead of every point. Option C (f(a)=f(b)) results from using endpoint equality instead of pointwise continuity. Option D (∀x∈I: f(x)=0) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0089', 8, 'For f:D→R, which symbolic statement represents continuity on its domain?', '∀x∈D: lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x)', '∃x∈D: lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x)', 'D={0}', '∀x∈D: f(x)=0', 'a', 1, 'Every point of D must satisfy the pointwise continuity condition.', 'A real-valued function is continuous when it is continuous at every point of its domain. Option B (∃x∈D: lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x)) results from requiring continuity at only one point instead of every point. Option C (D={0}) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option D (∀x∈D: f(x)=0) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0090', 8, 'For f(x)=7, what is the set of points where f is continuous?', 'R', '{7}', '{0}', '∅', 'a', 1, 'Constant functions are continuous at every real point.', 'The continuity set of a constant function is all of R. Option B ({7}) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C ({0}) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option D (∅) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0091', 8, 'For f(x)=x⁵, what is the set on which f is continuous?', 'R', '[0,∞)', '{5}', 'R\{0}', 'a', 1, 'Positive integer power functions are continuous at every real point.', 'The function x⁵ is a positive-integer power function, so it is continuous on R. Option B ([0,∞)) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option C ({5}) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (R\{0}) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0092', 8, 'For P(x)=2x⁴−3x²+x−6, what is its continuity set?', 'R', 'R\{0}', '(0,∞)', '{6}', 'a', 1, 'Use the continuity property of polynomials.', 'Polynomial functions are continuous at every point of R. Option B (R\{0}) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option C ((0,∞)) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option D ({6}) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0093', 8, 'For a>0, what is the continuity set of aˣ under the stated conditions?', 'R', '(0,∞)', 'R\{0}', '{a}', 'a', 1, 'Use the stated property of exponential functions.', 'The exponential function aˣ is continuous for all real x under the stated base condition. Option B ((0,∞)) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option C (R\{0}) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option D ({a}) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0094', 8, 'For b>0 and b≠1, what is the continuity set of log_b x?', '(0,∞)', '[0,∞)', 'R', 'R\{0}', 'a', 1, 'Use the domain condition for the logarithmic function.', 'The logarithmic function is considered for x>0, so its continuity set is (0,∞). Option B ([0,∞)) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option C (R) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (R\{0}) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0095', 8, 'For the rational function P(x)/Q(x), what condition is required at x=a for continuity?', 'Q(a)≠0', 'P(a)=0', 'Q(a)=0', 'P(a)=Q(a)', 'a', 1, 'Check whether the denominator is nonzero.', 'A rational function is continuous at points where Q(a)≠0. Option B (P(a)=0) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C (Q(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter. Option D (P(a)=Q(a)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0096', 8, 'What is the continuity set of an nth-root function?', 'its respective domain', '{0}', '(0,∞)', 'Z', 'a', 1, 'Use the stated continuity property of nth-root functions.', 'Nth-root functions are continuous in their respective domains. Option B ({0}) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option C ((0,∞)) results from choosing an incorrect continuity domain. Option D (Z) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0097', 8, 'Which symbolic set of conditions describes continuity on [a,b]?', 'lim(x→a−)f(x)=f(a); lim(x→b+)f(x)=f(b)', 'f(a)=f(b)', '∀x∈(a,b): lim(t→x)f(t)=f(x); lim(x→a+)f(x)=f(a); lim(x→b−)f(x)=f(b)', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a); lim(x→b)f(x)=f(b)', 'c', 2, 'Use interior continuity plus one-sided continuity directed into the interval at each endpoint.', 'Continuity on [a,b] requires continuity at every interior point, right continuity at a, and left continuity at b. Option A (lim(x→a−)f(x)=f(a); lim(x→b+)f(x)=f(b)) results from reversing the one-sided endpoint directions. Option B (f(a)=f(b)) results from using endpoint equality instead of pointwise continuity. Option D (lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a); lim(x→b)f(x)=f(b)) results from checking only the endpoint limits and omitting interior continuity.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0098', 8, 'If f is continuous on (2,9) and both endpoint limits exist, which interval is available for a continuous extension?', '[2,9]', '(2,9)', '[2,9)', '(2,9]', 'a', 2, 'Both existing endpoint limits allow both endpoints to be included.', 'The function can be extended continuously to the closed interval [2,9]. Option B ((2,9)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C ([2,9)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D ((2,9]) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0099', 8, 'At a joining point x=a of a piecewise function, which three quantities should be checked?', 'f(a), LHL, RHL', 'f(a), f(a+1), f(a−1)', 'LHL, f(a)', 'RHL', 'a', 2, 'Use all three continuity conditions at the joining point.', 'The continuity test uses the actual value f(a) together with the left- and right-hand limits. Option B (f(a), f(a+1), f(a−1)) results from checking neighboring values instead of one-sided limits. Option C (LHL, f(a)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (RHL) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0100', 8, 'What equality is required for a two-sided limit at x=a to exist?', 'LHL=RHL', 'LHL=0', 'RHL=0', 'LHL+RHL=1', 'a', 1, 'The two one-sided limits must agree.', 'A two-sided limit exists when the left-hand and right-hand limits are equal. Option B (LHL=0) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C (RHL=0) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (LHL+RHL=1) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0101', 8, 'If f(a) is undefined, which basic continuity requirement fails?', 'f(a)∈R', 'lim(x→a)f(x) exists', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a)', 'LHL=RHL', 'a', 1, 'Check the function value before checking the limits.', 'Continuity requires f(a) to be defined; if it is undefined, the first condition fails. Option B (lim(x→a)f(x) exists) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option C (lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (LHL=RHL) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0102', 8, 'If f(a) is defined but LHL≠RHL, which statement follows?', 'lim(x→a)f(x) does not exist', 'f(a)∉R', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a)', 'LHL=RHL', 'a', 1, 'A two-sided limit requires the left- and right-hand limits to agree.', 'If LHL and RHL differ, the two-sided limit does not exist. Option B (f(a)∉R) results from omitting a required condition. Option C (lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a)) results from replacing the required condition with a different mathematical condition. Option D (LHL=RHL) results from replacing the required condition with a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0103', 8, 'If lim(x→a)f(x)=L exists but f(a)≠L, which equality fails?', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a)', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=0', 'f(a)∈R', 'f(a)=a', 'a', 2, 'Compare the existing limit directly with f(a).', 'The third continuity condition fails because the existing limit does not equal the function value. Option B (lim(x→a)f(x)=0) results from omitting a required condition. Option C (f(a)∈R) results from replacing the required condition with a different mathematical condition. Option D (f(a)=a) results from replacing the required condition with a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0104', 8, 'Which equation can determine an unknown parameter k when continuity at x=a is required?', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a)', 'k=0', 'lim(x→a)f(x)=1', 'f(a)=a', 'a', 2, 'Set the limiting value equal to the function value.', 'Continuity can determine an unknown parameter by imposing lim(x→a)f(x)=f(a). Option B (k=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter. Option C (lim(x→a)f(x)=1) results from substituting an unrelated limit value. Option D (f(a)=a) results from confusing the function value with zero or the point label.'),
        ('mth11a-08-mcq-0105', 8, 'For f(x)=P(x)/Q(x), which denominator condition is necessary at x=a?', 'Q(a)≠0', 'Q(a)=0', 'P(a)=0', 'P(a)=Q(a)', 'a', 1, 'The quotient must be defined at the point.', 'The denominator must be nonzero at a, so Q(a)≠0. Option B (Q(a)=0) results from forcing a zero denominator or parameter. Option C (P(a)=0) results from substituting a different mathematical condition. Option D (P(a)=Q(a)) results from substituting a different mathematical condition.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0001', 9, 'Using the first-principle definition of the derivative, find d/dx of x^3. Here n=3.', '3x^2', '2x^3', 'x^3', '3x^1', 'a', 1, 'Use f′(x)=lim[h→0](f(x+h)−f(x))/h and simplify the resulting power difference.', 'Let f(x)=x^3. By first principle, f′(x)=lim[h→0](((x+h)^3−x^3)/h). Using the power-difference simplification gives 3x^2.
Option B: Using n−1 as the coefficient while leaving the exponent unchanged.
Option C: Failing to apply the power rule''s coefficient and exponent decrease.
