Option C: Using the supply value instead of its derivative.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0171', 9, 'If a position function is s(t), which expression represents the instantaneous rate of change of position with respect to time?', 's/t', 'ds/dt', 's·t', 'dt/ds', 'b', 1, 'Think of the derivative as the rate at which position changes with time.', 'The instantaneous rate of change is the derivative of position with respect to time: ds/dt. A is correct. B is an average-style ratio, C is the reciprocal differential ratio, and D is multiplication rather than differentiation.
Option A: uses s/t instead of ds/dt, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses s·t instead of ds/dt, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses dt/ds instead of ds/dt, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0172', 9, 'When motion has a direction, which quantity represents velocity in differential notation for position s with respect to time t?', 'ds·dt', 'dt/ds', 'ds/dt', 's+dt', 'b', 1, 'Use the derivative of position with respect to time.', 'Velocity is the rate of change of position with respect to time, ds/dt. A reverses the differential ratio, C adds unlike quantities, and D multiplies rather than differentiates.
Option A: uses ds·dt instead of dt/ds, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses ds/dt instead of dt/ds, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses s+dt instead of dt/ds, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0173', 9, 'For a function f differentiable at a, which limit represents f′(a)?', 'lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h', 'lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/a', 'lim[h→0](f(a+h)+f(a))/h', 'lim[h→a](f(a+h)−f(a))/h', 'a', 1, 'Use the difference quotient with h tending to zero.', 'By definition, f′(a)=lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h. B changes subtraction to addition, C divides by a instead of h, and D sends h to a rather than 0.
Option B: uses lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/a instead of lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses lim[h→0](f(a+h)+f(a))/h instead of lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses lim[h→a](f(a+h)−f(a))/h instead of lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0174', 9, 'If y=f(x), which notation is the Leibniz notation for its derivative?', 'dx/dy', 'dy/dx', 'y·dx', 'x·dy', 'a', 1, 'Read the notation as ''change in y divided by change in x.''', 'The derivative of y with respect to x is written dy/dx. A is the reciprocal differential notation, while C and D are products rather than the derivative notation.
Option B: uses dy/dx instead of dx/dy, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses y·dx instead of dx/dy, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses x·dy instead of dx/dy, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0175', 9, 'For the curve y=f(x), which expression gives the slope of the tangent at x=a?', 'a', '1/f′(a)', 'f(a)', 'f′(a)', 'd', 1, 'The derivative at a point equals the slope of the tangent there.', 'At x=a, the derivative f′(a) is the slope of the tangent line to y=f(x).
Option A: uses a instead of f′(a), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses 1/f′(a) instead of f′(a), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses f(a) instead of f′(a), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0176', 9, 'If y=u+v and both functions are differentiable, which formula gives dy/dx?', 'du/dx+dv/dx', '(v du/dx−u dv/dx)/v²', 'du/dx−dv/dx', 'u·dv/dx+v·du/dx', 'c', 1, 'Differentiate each addend separately.', 'For a sum, differentiate each term and add: dy/dx=du/dx+dv/dx. A is the difference rule, B is the product rule, and D is the quotient rule.
Option A: uses du/dx+dv/dx instead of du/dx−dv/dx, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses (v du/dx−u dv/dx)/v² instead of du/dx−dv/dx, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses u·dv/dx+v·du/dx instead of du/dx−dv/dx, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0177', 9, 'If y=u−v, which formula gives dy/dx?', 'du/dx−dv/dx', '(v du/dx−u dv/dx)/v²', 'du/dx+dv/dx', 'u−dv/dx', 'c', 1, 'Keep the minus sign between the derivatives.', 'For a difference, dy/dx=du/dx−dv/dx. A changes subtraction to addition, C leaves u undifferentiated, and D is the quotient rule.
Option A: uses du/dx−dv/dx instead of du/dx+dv/dx, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses (v du/dx−u dv/dx)/v² instead of du/dx+dv/dx, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses u−dv/dx instead of du/dx+dv/dx, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0178', 9, 'If y=7u−4v, which expression is dy/dx?', '7dv/dx−4du/dx', '7du/dx−4dv/dx', 'du/dx−dv/dx', '7u−4v', 'b', 1, 'Carry each constant through differentiation.', 'Constants remain as factors: dy/dx=7du/dx−4dv/dx. B drops the constant multipliers, C gives the original function, and D swaps the functions.
Option A: uses 7dv/dx−4du/dx instead of 7du/dx−4dv/dx, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses du/dx−dv/dx instead of 7du/dx−4dv/dx, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses 7u−4v instead of 7du/dx−4dv/dx, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0179', 9, 'If y=u·v, which formula gives dy/dx?', 'u(dv/dx)+v(du/dx)', '(du/dx)(dv/dx)', 'u(dv/dx)−v(du/dx)', 'u/v', 'a', 1, 'Differentiate one factor at a time while keeping the other factor.', 'For a product, dy/dx=u(dv/dx)+v(du/dx). B incorrectly subtracts the terms, C multiplies the derivatives only, and D is not a derivative formula.
Option B: uses (du/dx)(dv/dx) instead of u(dv/dx)+v(du/dx), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses u(dv/dx)−v(du/dx) instead of u(dv/dx)+v(du/dx), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses u/v instead of u(dv/dx)+v(du/dx), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0180', 9, 'If y=u·v·w, which expression gives dy/dx?', 'u(dv/dx)+v(du/dx)', 'uv(dw/dx)−uw(dv/dx)+vw(du/dx)', 'uvw', 'uv(dw/dx)+uw(dv/dx)+vw(du/dx)', 'd', 2, 'There are three terms, one for the derivative of each factor.', 'For three factors, differentiate each factor once while keeping the other two unchanged, then add the three terms. B omits the third factor contribution, C introduces an incorrect minus sign, and D is the original product.
Option A: uses u(dv/dx)+v(du/dx) instead of uv(dw/dx)+uw(dv/dx)+vw(du/dx), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses uv(dw/dx)−uw(dv/dx)+vw(du/dx) instead of uv(dw/dx)+uw(dv/dx)+vw(du/dx), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses uvw instead of uv(dw/dx)+uw(dv/dx)+vw(du/dx), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0181', 9, 'If y=u/v with v≠0, which formula gives dy/dx?', '(du/dx)/(dv/dx)', '(u dv/dx−v du/dx)/v²', 'u/v²', '(v du/dx−u dv/dx)/v²', 'd', 1, 'Start with denominator × derivative of numerator minus numerator × derivative of denominator.', 'The quotient rule is (v du/dx−u dv/dx)/v². B reverses the subtraction order, C divides the derivatives, and D omits both derivative contributions.
Option A: uses (du/dx)/(dv/dx) instead of (v du/dx−u dv/dx)/v², so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses (u dv/dx−v du/dx)/v² instead of (v du/dx−u dv/dx)/v², so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses u/v² instead of (v du/dx−u dv/dx)/v², so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0182', 9, 'For n∈N, what is d/dx(x^n)?', '(n−1)x^n', 'nx^n', 'x^(n+1)/(n+1)', 'nx^(n−1)', 'd', 1, 'Multiply by the exponent and reduce the exponent by one.', 'The standard derivative is nx^(n−1). B is an antiderivative form, C leaves the exponent unchanged, and D uses the wrong coefficient and exponent.
Option A: uses (n−1)x^n instead of nx^(n−1), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses nx^n instead of nx^(n−1), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses x^(n+1)/(n+1) instead of nx^(n−1), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0183', 9, 'What is d/dx(1/x)?', '1/x²', '−1/x', '−1/x²', '1/x', 'a', 1, 'Rewrite 1/x as x^(−1) and use the power rule.', 'Since 1/x=x^(−1), the standard derivative is −1/x². A loses the negative sign, C uses the wrong power, and D is the derivative of log x.
Option B: uses −1/x instead of 1/x², so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses −1/x² instead of 1/x², so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses 1/x instead of 1/x², so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0184', 9, 'What is d/dx(√x)?', '1/(2√x)', '−1/(2√x)', '2√x', '1/√x', 'a', 1, 'Write √x as x^(1/2).', 'Since √x=x^(1/2), its derivative is (1/2)x^(−1/2)=1/(2√x). B multiplies instead of differentiating, C misses the factor 1/2, and D introduces an incorrect negative sign.
Option B: uses −1/(2√x) instead of 1/(2√x), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses 2√x instead of 1/(2√x), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses 1/√x instead of 1/(2√x), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0185', 9, 'For a constant k, what is d/dx(k)?', 'x', 'k', '0', '1', 'b', 1, 'A constant does not change as x changes.', 'The derivative of a constant is 0. A and D leave variable dependence that is not present, while B is the derivative of x.
Option A: uses x instead of k, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses 0 instead of k, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses 1 instead of k, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0186', 9, 'What is d/dx(e^x)?', 'e^x', 'xe^(x−1)', 'e^(x−1)', '1/e^x', 'b', 1, 'The exponential e^x reproduces itself when differentiated.', 'The standard derivative of e^x is e^x. A incorrectly applies the power rule, C lowers the exponent, and D takes a reciprocal.
Option A: uses e^x instead of xe^(x−1), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses e^(x−1) instead of xe^(x−1), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses 1/e^x instead of xe^(x−1), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0187', 9, 'For a>0, what is d/dx(a^x)?', 'a^x', 'x a^(x−1)', 'a^(x−1)log a', 'a^x log a', 'a', 1, 'Use the exponential rule rather than the power rule.', 'For a>0, d(a^x)/dx=a^x log a. A omits log a, C applies the power rule, and D incorrectly lowers the exponent.
Option B: uses x a^(x−1) instead of a^x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses a^(x−1)log a instead of a^x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses a^x log a instead of a^x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0188', 9, 'What is d/dx(log x)?', '1/x', 'x', '−1/x²', 'log x', 'd', 1, 'Recall the standard logarithmic derivative.', 'The standard derivative is 1/x. A leaves the function unchanged, B is the derivative of x, and D is the derivative of 1/x.
Option A: uses 1/x instead of log x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses x instead of log x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses −1/x² instead of log x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0189', 9, 'If demand is D=f(P), which expression defines marginal demand?', 'D/P', 'dP/dD', 'P/D', 'dD/dP', 'b', 1, 'Differentiate demand with respect to price.', 'Marginal demand is the rate of change of demand with respect to price: dD/dP=f′(P). A reverses the rate, while C and D are ordinary ratios rather than derivatives.
Option A: uses D/P instead of dP/dD, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses P/D instead of dP/dD, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses dD/dP instead of dP/dD, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0190', 9, 'If supply is S=g(P), which expression defines marginal supply?', 'dS/dP', 'P/S', 'S/P', 'dP/dS', 'a', 1, 'Differentiate supply with respect to price.', 'Marginal supply is dS/dP=g′(P). B reverses the differential ratio, while C and D are ordinary ratios.
Option B: uses P/S instead of dS/dP, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses S/P instead of dS/dP, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses dP/dS instead of dS/dP, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0191', 9, 'If total cost is C=f(x), which expression defines marginal cost?', 'dC/dx', 'x/C', 'd x/dC', 'C/x', 'd', 1, 'Marginal cost is a derivative with respect to output.', 'Marginal cost is the rate of change of cost with respect to output: dC/dx=f′(x). A is average cost, C is its reciprocal, and D reverses the differential ratio.
Option A: uses dC/dx instead of C/x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses x/C instead of C/x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses d x/dC instead of C/x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0192', 9, 'If total cost is C for x goods, which formula gives average cost?', 'dC/dx', 'C+x', 'x/C', 'C/x', 'b', 1, 'Average means total cost divided by number of goods.', 'Average cost is cost per good, AC=C/x. A adds instead of divides, C reverses the ratio, and D is marginal cost.
Option A: uses dC/dx instead of C+x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses x/C instead of C+x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses C/x instead of C+x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0193', 9, 'If R(x) is total revenue from selling x units, which expression is marginal revenue?', 'dR/dx', 'R−x', 'R/x', 'd x/dR', 'c', 1, 'Marginal revenue is the rate of change of total revenue with respect to units.', 'Marginal revenue is the derivative R′(x)=dR/dx. A is an average-style ratio, C reverses the differential ratio, and D is not the definition.
Option A: uses dR/dx instead of R/x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses R−x instead of R/x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses d x/dR instead of R/x, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0194', 9, 'If P is price and D is quantity demanded, which formula gives total revenue?', 'P+D', 'P·D', 'D/P', 'P/D', 'a', 1, 'Revenue equals price per unit multiplied by quantity.', 'Total revenue is R=P·D. A adds the variables, C and D are ratios.
Option B: uses P·D instead of P+D, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses D/P instead of P+D, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses P/D instead of P+D, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0195', 9, 'If total revenue is R and quantity demanded is D, which expression gives average revenue?', 'R/D', 'R−D', 'R·D', 'D/R', 'c', 1, 'Average revenue is revenue per unit.', 'Average revenue is R/D, which equals P when R=P·D. A multiplies instead of dividing, C reverses the ratio, and D subtracts unrelated quantities.
Option A: uses R/D instead of R·D, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses R−D instead of R·D, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses D/R instead of R·D, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0196', 9, 'If R is total revenue and C is total cost, which expression gives total profit?', 'RC', 'R−C', 'C−R', 'R+C', 'd', 1, 'Profit is what remains after cost is subtracted from revenue.', 'Total profit is P=R−C. A adds cost to revenue, B reverses the subtraction, and D multiplies them.
Option A: uses RC instead of R+C, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses R−C instead of R+C, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses C−R instead of R+C, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0197', 9, 'If demand D depends on price P through a function f, which expression represents the demand function?', 'f(D)=P', 'P=f(D)', 'D=f(P)', 'D=f(x)', 'c', 1, 'Write demand as a function whose input is price.', 'If demand D is a function of price P, the notation is D=f(P).
Option A: uses f(D)=P instead of D=f(P), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses P=f(D) instead of D=f(P), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses D=f(x) instead of D=f(P), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0198', 9, 'If supply S is a function of price P, which notation represents the supply function?', 'g(S)=P²', 'S=g(P)', 'S=P+g', 'P=g(S)', 'b', 1, 'Write supply as a function of price.', 'Supply is expressed as S=g(P). B reverses the dependence, and C and D do not represent the stated functional form.
Option A: uses g(S)=P² instead of S=g(P), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option C: uses S=P+g instead of S=g(P), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses P=g(S) instead of S=g(P), so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0199', 9, 'Which condition is required for f to be differentiable at x=a under the stated definition?', 'f(a+h)=0', 'lim[h→a](f(a+h)−f(a))/h exists', 'lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h exists', 'f(a)=0', 'c', 2, 'Use the difference quotient in the definition of f′(a).', 'Differentiability at x=a requires the difference-quotient limit lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h to exist.
Option A: uses f(a+h)=0 instead of lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h exists, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses lim[h→a](f(a+h)−f(a))/h exists instead of lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h exists, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses f(a)=0 instead of lim[h→0](f(a+h)−f(a))/h exists, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.'),
        ('mth11a-09-mcq-0200', 9, 'If y=f(x), which first-principle formula represents f′(x)?', 'lim[h→0](f(x+h)+f(x))/h', 'lim[h→x](f(x+h)−f(x))/h', 'lim[h→0](f(x+h)−f(x))/h', 'lim[x→0](f(x+h)−f(x))/x', 'c', 1, 'Use the difference quotient with h tending to zero.', 'The first-principle formula is f′(x)=lim[h→0](f(x+h)−f(x))/h. B changes subtraction to addition, C uses the wrong limiting variable, and D uses the wrong limit target.
Option A: uses lim[h→0](f(x+h)+f(x))/h instead of lim[h→0](f(x+h)−f(x))/h, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option B: uses lim[h→x](f(x+h)−f(x))/h instead of lim[h→0](f(x+h)−f(x))/h, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.
Option D: uses lim[x→0](f(x+h)−f(x))/x instead of lim[h→0](f(x+h)−f(x))/h, so it applies the wrong formula or operation.')
    ) AS v (question_code, chapter_no, prompt, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, difficulty, hint, explanation)
) AS s
ON t.question_code = s.question_code
WHEN MATCHED AND (t.subject, t.chapter_no, t.prompt, t.option_a, t.option_b, t.option_c, t.option_d, t.correct_option, t.difficulty, t.hint, t.explanation) IS DISTINCT FROM (s.subject, s.chapter_no, s.prompt, s.option_a, s.option_b, s.option_c, s.option_d, s.correct_option, s.difficulty, s.hint, s.explanation) THEN
    UPDATE SET subject = s.subject, chapter_no = s.chapter_no, prompt = s.prompt, option_a = s.option_a, option_b = s.option_b, option_c = s.option_c, option_d = s.option_d, correct_option = s.correct_option, difficulty = s.difficulty, hint = s.hint, explanation = s.explanation
WHEN NOT MATCHED THEN
    INSERT (question_code, subject, chapter_no, prompt, option_a, option_b, option_c, option_d, correct_option, difficulty, hint, explanation) VALUES (s.question_code, s.subject, s.chapter_no, s.prompt, s.option_a, s.option_b, s.option_c, s.option_d, s.correct_option, s.difficulty, s.hint, s.explanation)
WHEN NOT MATCHED BY SOURCE AND t.subject = 'maths1' THEN
    DELETE;
